ties":109,"backingGap":0,"closingPaid":1,"closingOpened":true,"preservedMarker":7}},{"id":"proposed112-amount35","fundingMode":"proposed112","openingCash":112,"amount":35,"sourceWorksheetAdmitted":true,"expectation":"SOURCE_VALID_PARTIAL_EXPECTATION","reason":"Amount is strictly between 0 and opening invoice duty 100.","arithmeticStatus":"EXPECTED_SINGLE_STEP_VALUES","arithmetic":{"sellerReceipt":35,"feeRecipientReceipt":2,"grossEscrowDebit":37,"closingCash":75,"closingInvoiceDuty":65,"closingReserveDuty":10,"totalClosingDuties":75,"backingGap":0,"closingPaid":35,"closingOpened":true,"preservedMarker":7},"expectedSourcePartialEffects":{"sellerReceipt":35,"feeRecipientReceipt":2,"grossEscrowDebit":37,"closingCash":75,"closingInvoiceDuty":65,"closingReserveDuty":10,"totalClosingDuties":75,"backingGap":0,"closingPaid":35,"closingOpened":true,"preservedMarker":7}},{"id":"proposed112-amount99","fundingMode":"proposed112","openingCash":112,"amount":99,"sourceWorksheetAdmitted":true,"expectation":"SOURCE_VALID_PARTIAL_EXPECTATION","reason":"Amount is strictly between 0 and opening invoice duty 100.","arithmeticStatus":"EXPECTED_SINGLE_STEP_VALUES","arithmetic":{"sellerReceipt":99,"feeRecipientReceipt":2,"grossEscrowDebit":101,"closingCash":11,"closingInvoiceDuty":1,"closingReserveDuty":10,"totalClosingDuties":11,"backingGap":0,"closingPaid":99,"closingOpened":true,"preservedMarker":7},"expectedSourcePartialEffects":{"sellerReceipt":99,"feeRecipientReceipt":2,"grossEscrowDebit":101,"closingCash":11,"closingInvoiceDuty":1,"closingReserveDuty":10,"totalClosingDuties":11,"backingGap":0,"closingPaid":99,"closingOpened":true,"preservedMarker":7}},{"id":"proposed112-amount100","fundingMode":"proposed112","openingCash":112,"amount":100,"sourceWorksheetAdmitted":false,"expectation":"SOURCE_PARTIAL_REFUSED","reason":"Partial amount must be less than opening invoice duty; finish() is a separate action.","arithmeticStatus":"HYPOTHETICAL_ONLY_NO_SOURCE_VALID_PARTIAL","arithmetic":{"sellerReceipt":100,"feeRecipientReceipt":2,"grossEscrowDebit":102,"closingCash":10,"closingInvoiceDuty":0,"closingReserveDuty":10,"totalClosingDuties":10,"backingGap":0,"closingPaid":100,"closingOpened":true,"preservedMarker":7},"expectedSourcePartialEffects":null}],"limitations":["One step only. Each further partial has another 2 CASH source fee, so a sequence needs a further funding rule.","Amounts 0 and 100 are refused by partial against opening duty 100. Their arithmetic rows are hypothetical only.","finish() is a different source action: it transfers the closing invoice amount without the partial fee.","After source funding 110, partial(35), then finish(): cash is 8, invoice duty is present at zero, and reserve duty remains 10.","S12 contains no reserve-duty-return action.","Network fees, collateral, claim-output min-ADA, and the V2 thread reserve are separate from the worksheet’s CASH source fees."]},"security":{"format":"moriarty-webs-page-security-copy/1","statusScope":{"status":"PROPOSED local draft security presentation strings","author":"role06 security copy; requested Opus 5.5 at HIGH effort. Actual provider, model and effort are recorded only by the wrapper.","scope":"Security and evidence wording for the local Webs and Strands explanatory page draft only, used together with SECURITY-ERRATA.json ERR-01 to ERR-28. Not security approval, mechanism adoption, implementation, product release, publication approval, operator readiness or native authority.","review":"Newly authored copy. It is not a review of any page. Fresh review of the actual integrated page remains pending: a nonauthor Muse editorial/result review and a separate Opus review of the exact final security wording. Any changed byte needs fresh applicable review.","sourceReadingCaveat":"Every V2 consumer or claim-policy description here is an Opus source-level reading of unadopted, uncompiled Aiken/Rust files. It is not observed compiled behavior and not exact-source evidence for the recorded S07 roundtrip or the S08 compiled C2-wrapper (K10) runs: no supplied record pins those binaries or compiled bytes to the reviewed source. The four-piece settle implementation and byte/type helpers were not supplied to the reviewer; four-piece reuse rests on S09 and source comments.","integrationRule":"Nonsecurity authors may quote a card, rule or string exactly, with its assumptions, evidenceStage and sourceRefs adjacent or one disclosure away, or omit it. Do not drop an assumption, upgrade a stage, merge cards into a broader claim, or add a security conclusion. New security wording needs an Opus referral.","stageLabels":{"RECORDED":"Recorded observation in a supplied S07/S08 record; local and scoped, not ledger acceptance.","V2_SOURCE_READING":"Opus reading of unadopted, uncompiled V2 source; not observed behavior.","CONDITIONAL_ARGUMENT":"A01/A02 conditional source-design argument only (T1 modulo premises).","PROPOSED":"Design proposal; not adopted, implemented or enforced.","OPEN":"Unresolved; nothing in this packet closes it.","PREMISE":"Cardano ledger rule assumed; not contained in the sources.","ILLUSTRATION":"Page-local example data or state; no proof, transaction or ledger meaning."}},"maturity":{"title":"Current maturity","body":"Moriarty Webs is a concept under review. Supplied records include one current-Rust invoice-partial Groth16 roundtrip (S07) and seven finite local compiled-C2-wrapper cases (S08: one acceptance and six mutation rejections), with a single-party test setup whose operator can forge proofs and caller-fabricated proof contexts. No on-chain transaction is recorded. Only a conditional lowering argument is adopted. V2 consumer descriptions on this page are Opus readings of unadopted, uncompiled source, not observed behavior or exact-source correspondence to those runs. General Web integration, genuine intent authorization, onchain settlement and all-family compatibility remain unestablished in this packet. Local VM cost is not ledger cost. Every interactive example on this page is an illustration.","sourceRefs":["S07 /scope","S08 /predicate","S08 /scopeLimitations","A01 /scope","A03 §0","ERR-05","ERR-17","ERR-19","ERR-20","CL-11","CL-12","CL-13","CL-14","CL-15"]},"cards":[{"id":"proof-meaning","title":"What a verifying proof shows","body":"Under an honest setup and Groth16 knowledge soundness, a verifying proof shows the prover knew a witness satisfying the compiled constraints for its two public inputs. Linking those inputs to statement bytes additionally rests on the in-circuit BLAKE2b gadget and on BLAKE2b collision resistance. It never authenticates those bytes. A proof over supplied values does not make them an authenticated ledger fact or a true outside observation. A concrete execution proof, a universal property certificate and an authenticated ledger claim are different assertions; the latter two are not established Strand kinds.","assumptions":["The current class-0 test setup is forgeable by its operator, so no production security claim follows.","Whether the compiled constraints and verifying key match your source is OPEN (T3: compiled circuit/VK and BLAKE2b gadget correspondence).","Outside facts keep their named issuer, predicate, authentication path and freshness limits.","Reference-input reads are PROPOSED: the validator must read the datum itself and authenticate its origin. PREMISE: being unspent at application shows presence only, with no exclusivity or history. A validity interval neither dates nor authenticates a datum.","SEC06-P06 PROPOSED; still needs real-context stale-sequence and expired-attestation cases, changed-reference-datum and forged-origin negatives, N5/N7–N9 at V, and a separately scoped real-context proof."],"evidenceStage":"Assumption-bound Groth16 meaning; T3 OPEN; reference-input path PROPOSED","sourceRefs":["ERR-16","ERR-17","ERR-01","ERR-11","CL-27","CL-45","CL-49","S07 /limitations/universalPropertyCertificate","S07 /limitations/class0ControllerCanForge","SEC06-P06 PROPOSED"]},{"id":"recorded-evidence","title":"What has actually been recorded","body":"Evidence layers (DEMO ONLY): H ordinary host tests captured (K4H, S04; fresh actual-result review pending) | N 1 local roundtrip (S07) | V 1 local C2 wrapper, 7 cases (S08) | P none. One genuine proof was accepted by a compiled C2 wrapper in a local VM with fabricated context (S08); no on-chain transaction is recorded. S08 /cases/0 records cpu 2222340191 and mem 205312 for the whole compiled C2 Issue evaluation, including its pairing; it is neither an isolated verification cost nor ledger ExUnits, fee or transaction fit. Every recorded proof context in the supplied sources is caller-fabricated (S07, S08).","assumptions":["No supplied record pins the recorded runs to the later V2 source bytes; V2 readings elsewhere on this page are not evidence about these runs.","Named runtime guards behind the six S08 rejections were not instrumented.","All 20 retained S02 families are requirements, not supported ports. The 235 catalogue IDs are pre-dedup review identifiers, not accepted or proven properties; the original 42/52 lists remain unrecovered.","Moriarty's broader target remains Midnight ZKIRv3 and native proof infrastructure; Cardano is an additional experiment and does not qualify Midnight. Preview-first and later Preprod end-to-end acceptance remain required for claimed targets.","SEC06-P08 PROPOSED (developer trust report); still needs a DEMO ONLY S07/S08 report with these qualified layers, fresh nonsecurity wording review and fresh security review. No new evidence is inferred from this layer wording."],"evidenceStage":"RECORDED (local, scoped)","sourceRefs":["ERR-18","ERR-17","ERR-05","ERR-19","CL-11","CL-12","CL-13","CL-14","CL-31","CL-32","CL-33","CL-34","S07 /scope","S08 /cases","S08 /scopeLimitations","SEC06-P08 PROPOSED"]},{"id":"setup-keys","title":"Setup and keys","body":"The current test setup can be forged by its operator; whether the verifying key matches the compiled relation is OPEN. Groth16 needs a separate setup per distinct compiled circuit (R1CS matrices), not per logical predicate. In the current Rust source the predicate digest is a circuit constant, so each distinct relation identity is a distinct circuit. The required total setup count is unknown; predicate, catalogue and family counts do not determine it. No production ceremony is recorded in the supplied sources, and none is proposed here.","assumptions":["A future universal circuit or Midnight's universal PLONK/KZG SRS would change the comparison (PROPOSED or target-specific).","V2 SOURCE READING: catalogue VK digests are fixed at genesis and carried unchanged, and Close requires zero balances and duties, so any key change needs a PROPOSED key migration action that carries residual duties.","Intent revocation (Revoke) advances seq; it is not key migration.","SEC06-P07 PROPOSED; still needs an old-VK-refused-after-migration negative, a setup-class report and independent review of any future ceremony."],"evidenceStage":"RECORDED limitation (forgeable test setup); V2 SOURCE READING; total setup count UNKNOWN","sourceRefs":["ERR-14","ERR-28","ERR-17","CL-14","CL-32","CL-43","SEC06-P07 PROPOSED"]},{"id":"binding-chain","title":"What ties a Strand to its step","body":"V2 SOURCE READING: tag, family, predicate_digest, shape_digest and body_len are circuit constants; the context fields vk_digest through intents_digest (bytes 112..339, including claimant_vkh) are free booleanized witness bits that are hashed, not interpreted. The consumer, not the circuit, ties those fields to the transaction: the settling validator rebuilds each statement from the transaction and the spent thread state, so a claim made for another thread step, catalogue, claimant or intent set does not match. Claimant binding happens at consumption, not at issuance: C2 Issue does not compare the claimant with the statement, so a mismatched envelope can be minted but cannot settle. This source-level binding is separate from T3, which stays OPEN.","assumptions":["The class-0 setup operator can forge a proof for any digest and any claimant. The claimant is prover-chosen bytes, not an authenticated prover identity.","Builders select claim inputs; the ledger spends each claim once (PREMISE). A copied proof can be re-issued with the same claimant and a new expiry; only that claimant can release it.","Catalogue program_hash and source_hash are visible, covered by catalogue_digest and signed in every owner intent. No on-chain check links them to predicate_digest or vk_digest: that link is a CATALOGUE-AUTHOR ASSERTION and VK-to-matrix is OPEN. Exact predicate-digest field coverage depends on identity.rs, which the reviewer did not read.","The statement's network byte is declared by the catalogue and not compared with the executing ledger; separation across networks rests on outref uniqueness (PREMISE).","SEC06-P03 and SEC06-P04 PROPOSED; still need competing-claimant and same-claimant duplicate negatives, second-party source→relation→predicate reproduction, refusal-list coverage, N5/N7–N9, a T3 method, reviewed T2/T3 and F07–F10, a non-test setup, and cross-network replay analysis if the network byte is relied on."],"evidenceStage":"V2 SOURCE READING (unadopted, uncompiled); T3 OPEN","sourceRefs":["ERR-02","ERR-03","ERR-13","ERR-07","ERR-16","CL-36","CL-39","CL-40","CL-45","CL-46","SEC06-P03 PROPOSED","SEC06-P04 PROPOSED"]},{"id":"issuance-route","title":"Where proofs are checked: the issuance route","body":"In the reviewed V2 source, each proof is checked when its claim is issued: the C2 claim policy checks a Groth16 proof and mints one claim output per Issue transaction. Claims must exist before settlement. The settlement transaction consumes them, recomputes each statement and compares it with the digest checked at issuance; it does not verify the proof again. Each consumed claim needs its claimant's signature on the settlement transaction and the burn of its token. Structurally, n required Strands mean n Issue transactions plus one settlement, excluding one-time genesis and any post-expiry reclaim. These counts are readings of uncompiled source, not measurements.","assumptions":["Each claim output locks min-ADA from issuance until settlement or claimant-signed reclaim after the claimant-chosen expiry. Several envelopes can exist per statement digest.","Safe but not live: any catalogue account may Revoke (advancing seq), and a competing settlement may spend the outref, orphaning issued claims and locking their value until expiry. Earlier reclaim is PROPOSED (OPEN).","Batch issuance needs a C2 change (PROPOSED). Proof-at-consumption is a different PROPOSED design needing a new redeemer and in-settlement pairings, with size and budget unmeasured. Mempool chaining is a node premise outside the sources.","V2 source already refuses two claims for one consumed outref; S09's role-level refusal is an unadopted proposal and not deduplication permission.","Owner intents are signed before proving; claimant settlement signatures are a later, distinct event. Proof collection or claim-output creation executes no effect.","Still needs orphan-after-Revoke, competing-settlement and post-expiry reclaim cases, and a qualified complete-path measurement of both verification placements."],"evidenceStage":"V2 SOURCE READING (structural); not observed onchain","sourceRefs":["ERR-04","ERR-06","ERR-07","ERR-12","ERR-25","ERR-26","ERR-27","CL-02","CL-30","CL-47","CL-50","SEC06-P04 PROPOSED"]},{"id":"web-completeness","title":"Complete and incomplete Webs","body":"PROPOSED: a Web settles only when every Strand its contract requires is present. An incomplete Web has no authority over thread effects. On the issuance route its already-issued claim outputs exist on-chain, lock value, and remain releasable only by their claimant. In the first proposed supported profile, the consumer refuses universal property certificates, authenticated-history claims, private handoff, recursive parents and multiparent joins as UNSUPPORTED; they remain required Web tracks, each needing its own trust path and evidence before any profile accepts it.","assumptions":["All-or-nothing acceptance and a consumer-derived required set are PROPOSED; no Web consumer, second genuine relation or general cardinality exists in this packet.","Depicting several Strand roles does not establish separate relations or producers. A whole-execution proof may cover several claim specifications; naming source expressions fee or liability does not create independent proof producers.","A01 adopts only conditional T1: the all-role conjunction equals whole-action Core over one decoded body, modulo premise G including output well-formedness, a covering WebPlan, whole-action compilation first and the same field. There is no implementation, no T2, no T3 and no full WP migration (F07–F10); builder reliance is not permitted.","OPEN: whether a consumer can enforce a fully authorized partial financial step that carries every proof that step requires, distinct from a missing proof.","SEC06-P01 and SEC06-P02 PROPOSED; still need N1/N12 and unknown-class refusal, a report-level test that an incomplete set never renders as accepted, N1–N4/N6/N11/N14 at H/V, a wrong-action-index case, a required-set display, separate future-profile designs and fresh security review of reused V2 mechanisms."],"evidenceStage":"PROPOSED; CONDITIONAL_ARGUMENT for T1 only","sourceRefs":["ERR-01","ERR-15","ERR-10","ERR-20","ERR-27","CL-03","CL-15","CL-16","CL-17","CL-21","CL-37","CL-38","SEC06-P01 PROPOSED","SEC06-P02 PROPOSED"]},{"id":"effects-authority","title":"Effects, allocation and authority","body":"V2 SOURCE READING: owner intents sign per-asset gross_cap, fee_cap, net_floor and liability_cap for a beneficiary, a validity window, nonce = seq and the target predicate, VK and shape digests. Duties change only through recorded accruals and discharges, and a thread with a nonzero duty cannot close. The thread datum carries the complete custody snapshot, which custody must equal exactly. Actual output allocation is the consuming validator's job; a proof can attest arithmetic only over values the validator recomputes. A profile chooses either strict one-output-per-obligation or a consumer-recomputed amount partition that admits coalesced payments, with every obligation allocated exactly once.","assumptions":["The source strict-output rule covers redeemers of one S2 script hash only, not other scripts or S2 versions. No input value may count toward two obligations; ledger single-spend alone is not a value partition.","Circuit-level NotBound/TouchedOnly limits remain. A general multi-UTxO authenticated frame, role sets, cross-script allocation and partial-completion rules are PROPOSED.","No genuine intent signature is observed in the supplied proof records.","A supplied approved=true is consented to by the effect-derived required signers, and by a listed advancer if any; that is the debited account's consent, not a separate governance decision. A distinct approver must be a required signer, an advancer or an authenticated input.","SEC06-P05 PROPOSED; still needs double-satisfaction negatives across scripts and S2 versions, a coalesced positive under partition, fee-over-cap and dropped-duty cases, the approver-coverage lint, a genuine validator-checked signature in real context, and independent custody/duty/backing accounting with a backing-policy negative."],"evidenceStage":"V2 SOURCE READING; allocation policy PROPOSED per profile","sourceRefs":["ERR-08","ERR-09","ERR-10","ERR-23","ERR-24","CL-22","CL-25","CL-41","CL-48","CL-51","SEC06-P05 PROPOSED"]},{"id":"duties-backing-fees","title":"Duties, backing and fees","body":"S12 and S13 source behavior requires non-negative custody, not full backing of liabilities. S12 funds escrow at 110: partial(35) debits 37 and leaves 73 against duties of 75 (invoice 65, reserve 10), which is 2 short. Funding 112 would back one partial; each further partial charges another 2 that no duty reflects. That backing is a PROPOSED application policy. S12 has no extra transfer and no reserve-duty-return action; after finish, escrow holds 8 against reserve duty 10, and the nonzero reserve duty blocks V2 Close. S13 allocate(true,23,7,2) needs at least 23 A and 4 B for non-negative custody; 25 A and 10 B is PROPOSED full backing, and S13 has no action discharging allocation_reserve.","assumptions":["Source-only arithmetic, not execution. The V2 Close zero-duty condition is an uncompiled-source reading.","Even under the proposed 112 policy the reserve duty stays 10 and Close stays blocked; discharge or migration needs a program change.","S13 units is checked only arithmetically (23 = 3×7 + 2) and is never debited.","A program-asset fee is not debt repayment and not a network fee. Network fees, collateral, claim-output min-ADA and the V2 thread reserve (lovelace returned on Close) are separate funding items; a reserve duty is a program liability.","Delivery evidence and dispute rules remain named assumptions. Still needs independent open/partial/finish/reserve-return accounting and a backing-policy negative."],"evidenceStage":"Source arithmetic reading; full backing PROPOSED","sourceRefs":["ERR-21","ERR-22","ERR-25","ERR-28","CL-19","CL-20","CL-25","CL-26","CL-52","S12 actions","S13 action allocate","SEC06-P05 PROPOSED"]}],"glossaryClarifications":[{"term":"Strand","clarification":"An individual proof with its claim, statement and binding metadata. Before a proof exists, say requirement or planned proof. A signed owner intent, UTxO, claim output, validator, catalogue entry, pattern or predicate is not a Strand.","sourceRefs":["CL-04","ERR-28","A04 /glossary"]},{"term":"Web","clarification":"PROPOSED: the contract-fixed set of required Strands for one transition, accepted all-or-nothing by the consuming validator in a supported profile. Profiles refuse unsupported classes; certificates, authenticated history, private handoff, recursion and bounded multiparent remain required future tracks.","sourceRefs":["ERR-01","CL-03"]},{"term":"Claim specification","clarification":"The reusable type of a Strand; not itself a proof. Reusing a specification does not reuse a proof for changed state.","sourceRefs":["CL-05","ERR-28"]},{"term":"Claim output","clarification":"V2 SOURCE READING: a C2 UTxO holding one token named H(envelope), minted after the claim policy's Groth16 check. It confers no authority by itself and is neither the proof nor its validator.","sourceRefs":["ERR-28","A04 /glossary"]},{"term":"Verified at issuance / at consumption","clarification":"Issuance is the reviewed V2 route. Verification at consumption is PROPOSED and needs a new redeemer and in-settlement pairings.","sourceRefs":["ERR-04","ERR-12","ERR-26"]},{"term":"Thread reserve / reserve duty","clarification":"Thread reserve is V2 lovelace returned on Close. A reserve duty is a program liability such as S12 reserve or S13 allocation_reserve.","sourceRefs":["ERR-28"]},{"term":"Intent revocation (Revoke) / key migration","clarification":"Revoke advances seq to cancel outstanding intents and can orphan issued claims. Key migration (VK change) is PROPOSED; V2 catalogues are immutable.","sourceRefs":["ERR-06","ERR-14","ERR-28"]},{"term":"Allocation policy","clarification":"A per-profile choice between strict one-output-per-obligation (reviewed V2 scope: one S2 script hash) and a consumer-recomputed amount partition.","sourceRefs":["ERR-08","ERR-23"]},{"term":"thread (internal identifier)","clarification":"thread is a V2 source identifier for the consumer's state instance (thread id, thread effects, thread reserve). It is not a public synonym for Strand; public vocabulary is Web and Strand.","sourceRefs":["BRIEF item 1","ERR-28"]},{"term":"Assembled (page state)","clarification":"ILLUSTRATION: every illustrated requirement has an illustrated Strand. It is not proof verification, acceptance or settlement.","sourceRefs":["PLAN interfaces note","ERR-15"]},{"term":"Settlement","clarification":"Actual target-ledger acceptance of the authorized effects under the applicable conditions. Nothing on this page settles anything.","sourceRefs":["CONCEPT glossary"]},{"term":"CATALOGUE-AUTHOR ASSERTION","clarification":"A link stated by the catalogue author and not checked on-chain, here between program_hash/source_hash and predicate_digest/vk_digest.","sourceRefs":["ERR-13"]},{"term":"PREMISE","clarification":"A Cardano ledger rule assumed by the reasoning but not contained in the sources, for example single spending of an outref.","sourceRefs":["A03 §0"]}],"diagramRules":[{"id":"DR-01","rule":"When the explorer draws several illustrated Strand roles (for example three), show the label: Illustrated roles. Separate relations, their proof producers and a required-set consumer are unestablished. Drawing a role never implies an existing producer.","sourceRefs":["CONCEPT useful job","CL-17","SEC06-P02 PROPOSED","ERR-10"]},{"id":"DR-02","rule":"Only proof-bearing items are drawn as Strands. Draw signed owner intents, UTxOs, claim outputs, validators, catalogue entries, patterns and predicates with a distinct shape and legend entry, never with the Strand symbol.","sourceRefs":["CL-04","ERR-28"]},{"id":"DR-03","rule":"Incomplete state text: Incomplete required set (illustration). Under the PROPOSED all-or-nothing rule it has no authority over thread effects. Optional second line: On the issuance route, already-issued claim outputs could still exist on-chain, lock min-ADA and be releasable only by their claimant. Never use verified, valid, accepted, settled, success, a check icon or a settlement color for this state.","sourceRefs":["ERR-15","SEC06-P01 PROPOSED"]},{"id":"DR-04","rule":"Restored state text: Illustrative set assembled. Not proof verification, not acceptance, not settlement. No success badge, check icon or celebratory motion, and visually distinct from any recorded-evidence treatment.","sourceRefs":["ERR-15","PLAN interfaces note"]},{"id":"DR-05","rule":"A connection from a Strand to its consumer means required by (PROPOSED). Do not label it verifies, executes or triggers, and do not depict automatic output triggering, chaining, aggregation, recursion or batching.","sourceRefs":["CL-06","CL-07","ERR-04"]},{"id":"DR-06","rule":"Issuance-route diagram order: owner intents signed → proof production → one Issue per Strand (C2 checks the proof, mints one claim output, locks min-ADA) → one settlement (consumes claim outputs, recomputes statements against the issuance-checked digest, each claimant signs, tokens burned) → separate observation of inclusion, finality and readback. Label it V2 SOURCE READING, structural, not observed. Draw proof-at-consumption only as a separate PROPOSED alternative.","sourceRefs":["ERR-04","ERR-12","ERR-25","ERR-26","CL-47"]},{"id":"DR-07","rule":"Context provenance: label illustrated context values as supplied, unauthenticated unless a named authentication path is shown. No freshness badge from a validity interval, no network-authentication badge from the catalogue network byte, and the claimant shown as prover-chosen bytes, not an authenticated identity.","sourceRefs":["ERR-03","ERR-07","ERR-11","ERR-19"]},{"id":"DR-08","rule":"Illustrative identifiers, digests and counts must read as obvious placeholders (for example illustrative-statement-01), never as real-looking hex. Recorded values such as the S07/S08 VK digest prefix or the K10 cpu/mem figures appear only in the evidence drawer with their recorded scope.","sourceRefs":["ERR-05","ERR-18","BRIEF item 2"]},{"id":"DR-09","rule":"Financial partial progress (a duty remaining) and a missing required proof are separate states with separate controls. The invoice worksheet never changes Web completeness, and no proof badge appears in the worksheet.","sourceRefs":["CL-21","SEC06-P01 PROPOSED","SEC06-P05 PROPOSED"]},{"id":"DR-10","rule":"Every security-relevant state (incomplete, illustrative assembled, PROPOSED, OPEN, recorded) is conveyed in text, not by color or motion alone.","sourceRefs":["ERR-15"]}],"invoiceRules":[{"id":"IR-01","rule":"Opening is a source reading, not an executed result: S12 open(90,10) gives invoice duty 100 (face = 90 + 10), reserve duty 10 and escrow 110 CASH. The buyer pays the 110 transfer plus a 3 CASH program-asset fee, a gross debit of 113. CASH is the S12 fixture asset (network oracle-unit), not a Cardano asset.","sourceRefs":["S12 action open","CL-19"]},{"id":"IR-02","rule":"Slider domain: integers 1 to 99 inclusive. S12 partial requires amount > 0 and amount < opening, and opening is 100.","sourceRefs":["S12 action partial"]},{"id":"IR-03","rule":"0 and 100 are refusals, not accepted source actions. If shown as test cases, label them Refused: requires amount > 0, and Refused: requires amount < opening (100). Paying the whole balance is the separate finish action, which charges no fee and is not part of this one-partial worksheet.","sourceRefs":["S12 action partial","S12 action finish"]},{"id":"IR-04","rule":"Source behavior (S12, not executed): escrow debit = amount + 2; escrow cash = 110 − amount − 2; invoice duty = 100 − amount; reserve duty = 10; total duties = 110 − amount. After one partial the source path is always 2 short of total duties. Example amount 35: debit 37, cash 73, duties 65 + 10 = 75. Never label this path fully backed.","sourceRefs":["ERR-22","CL-19","CL-52"]},{"id":"IR-05","rule":"PROPOSED full-backing view: funding 112, cash = 112 − amount − 2, which equals total duties after one partial. Label it PROPOSED application policy. S12 open funds exactly face + reserve and has no extra transfer, so 112 is not an S12 action, and the source enforces only non-negative custody, not backing.","sourceRefs":["ERR-22","CL-19","CL-26"]},{"id":"IR-06","rule":"Funding 112 backs one partial only. Each further partial charges escrow another 2 that no duty reflects, so later fees create further gaps unless a further rule and funding are adopted.","sourceRefs":["ERR-22"]},{"id":"IR-07","rule":"The 2 CASH fee goes to fees and is not debt repayment; the invoice duty falls only by the payment amount. Network fees, collateral, claim-output min-ADA and the V2 thread reserve are separate, unknown here and not included in the worksheet.","sourceRefs":["ERR-25","ERR-28","CL-19"]},{"id":"IR-08","rule":"Reserve: S12 has no reserve-duty-return action, so reserve duty 10 remains. If finish is mentioned, the source path leaves cash 8 against reserve duty 10 (the proposed 112 path leaves 10 against 10), and the nonzero reserve duty blocks V2 Close either way. Never show closed, complete or reserve returned.","sourceRefs":["ERR-22","CL-20","CL-52"]},{"id":"IR-09","rule":"The worksheet is a local arithmetic lesson. It does not execute Moriarty or a proof and creates no transaction or ledger state. A permitted partial step is intended to carry every proof required for that step (PROPOSED; consumer enforcement OPEN); remaining duty is not a missing proof.","sourceRefs":["BRIEF item 3","CL-21","SEC06-P01 PROPOSED","SEC06-P05 PROPOSED"]},{"id":"IR-10","rule":"Required visible labels: Source behavior (S12, not executed) and PROPOSED full-backing policy, plus the explicit 2-short line on the source path.","sourceRefs":["ERR-22"]}],"forbiddenPhrases":[{"phrase":"trustless","instead":"State the named assumptions (honest setup, T3 OPEN, forgeable test setup).","sourceRefs":["A04 /notApprovedPhrases"]},{"phrase":"proven on Cardano","instead":"One genuine proof was accepted by a compiled C2 wrapper in a local VM with fabricated context (S08); no on-chain transaction is recorded.","sourceRefs":["ERR-17"]},{"phrase":"secure, security guarantee, guaranteed, audited, security-approved (unscoped)","instead":"Use the card text with its assumptions and evidence stage.","sourceRefs":["A03 §1","A03 §11"]},{"phrase":"verified, valid, accepted or settled for an assembled or restored illustration","instead":"Illustrative set assembled. Not proof verification, not acceptance, not settlement.","sourceRefs":["ERR-15"]},{"phrase":"Bring proof with the transaction","instead":"Bring the required proofs to the transition.","sourceRefs":["ERR-26"]},{"phrase":"settlement verifies the proofs","instead":"Settlement recomputes each statement and compares it with the digest checked at issuance.","sourceRefs":["ERR-12"]},{"phrase":"two extra transactions per Strand","instead":"One Issue transaction per required Strand plus one settlement (structural source reading).","sourceRefs":["ERR-04"]},{"phrase":"a partial Web is off-chain only","instead":"An incomplete Web has no authority over thread effects; already-issued claim outputs can exist on-chain and lock value.","sourceRefs":["ERR-15"]},{"phrase":"each output credited to exactly one obligation (unscoped)","instead":"Under this profile's allocation rule, no payment can be counted toward two obligations.","sourceRefs":["ERR-08"]},{"phrase":"cannot be replayed on another network (as a statement property)","instead":"Separation across networks rests on outref uniqueness; the network byte is catalogue-declared.","sourceRefs":["ERR-07"]},{"phrase":"fresh as of the validity interval","instead":"A validity interval bounds when the transaction may apply; it does not date or authenticate a datum.","sourceRefs":["ERR-11"]},{"phrase":"program identity is invisible","instead":"Program and source hashes are visible and signed; their link to predicate/VK is a CATALOGUE-AUTHOR ASSERTION.","sourceRefs":["ERR-13"]},{"phrase":"no production ceremony exists (unbounded)","instead":"No production ceremony is recorded in the supplied sources.","sourceRefs":["ERR-14"]},{"phrase":"every context is fabricated (unbounded)","instead":"Every recorded proof context in the supplied sources is caller-fabricated (S07, S08).","sourceRefs":["ERR-19"]},{"phrase":"one setup per predicate, or a setup count derived from predicate, catalogue or family counts","instead":"Setup is per distinct compiled circuit; the required total is unknown.","sourceRefs":["ERR-14"]},{"phrase":"claimant authenticated, or prover identity","instead":"The claimant is prover-chosen bytes bound at consumption in source.","sourceRefs":["ERR-03"]},{"phrase":"governance-approved, from approved=true","instead":"Consent of the effect-derived required signers, not a separate governance decision.","sourceRefs":["ERR-09","ERR-24"]},{"phrase":"fully backed or backing enforced, for the S12 source path","instead":"The source checks only non-negative custody; full backing is a PROPOSED policy.","sourceRefs":["ERR-21","ERR-22"]},{"phrase":"reserve returned, invoice closed, lifecycle complete","instead":"S12 has no reserve-duty-return action; the nonzero reserve duty blocks V2 Close.","sourceRefs":["ERR-22"]},{"phrase":"verified on-chain, or ledger cost, for the S08 local VM figure","instead":"Whole local C2 Issue evaluation; not isolated verification or ledger ExUnits.","sourceRefs":["ERR-05"]},{"phrase":"235 proven properties; supports all 20 families","instead":"235 pre-dedup review identifiers; 20 families retained as requirements.","sourceRefs":["CL-31","CL-32"]},{"phrase":"cheaper, faster, lower fees","instead":"No advantage is claimed; no measurement exists.","sourceRefs":["CL-29","CL-30"]}],"footer":"Security presentation copy by role06 (requested Opus 5.5 HIGH) for a PROPOSED local draft of the Webs and Strands page, used with SECURITY-ERRATA.json. V2 descriptions are Opus source-level readings of unadopted, uncompiled code; recorded results are local and scoped, with a forgeable test setup and fabricated contexts. No security approval, adoption, release or publication is implied. Fresh review of the final page is pending."},"learning":{"format":"moriarty-webs-page-learning/1","status":"PROPOSED_LOCAL_PAGE_CONTRIBUTION","journey":[{"id":"define","title":"Define one financial action","body":"Choose a target/profile. Name exact assets, recipients, fee limits and continuing duties.","status":"PROPOSED"},{"id":"compare","title":"Compare independent effects","body":"Separate source behavior from proposed policy. Keep the arithmetic worksheet independent of the proof illustration.","status":"PROPOSED"},{"id":"map","title":"Map requirements to evidence","body":"Identify each claim specification, statement and proof producer. Missing mappings remain visible.","status":"PROPOSED"},{"id":"observe","title":"Keep each stage visible","body":"Distinguish preparation, authorization, proof production, assembly, submission and actual effect readback.","status":"PROPOSED"}],"journeySourceRefs":[{"label":"Proposed staged developer journeys","document":"DEVELOPER-EXPERIENCE.md","pointer":"Journey A: build a first dapp action","pointerType":"markdown-heading"}],"faq":[{"id":"strand","question":"What is a Strand?","answer":"An individual proof with its claim, statement and binding metadata. Before a proof exists, say requirement or planned proof. A signed owner intent, UTxO, claim output, validator, catalogue entry, pattern or predicate is not a Strand.","sourceRefs":[{"label":"Opus role06 wording","document":"SECURITY-COPY.json","pointer":"/glossaryClarifications/0/clarification","pointerType":"json-pointer"}],"wording":"EXACT_OPUS_QUOTE"},{"id":"assembled","question":"Does restoring a Strand verify the Web?","answer":"ILLUSTRATION: every illustrated requirement has an illustrated Strand. It is not proof verification, acceptance or settlement.","sourceRefs":[{"label":"Opus role06 wording","document":"SECURITY-COPY.json","pointer":"/glossaryClarifications/9/clarification","pointerType":"json-pointer"}],"wording":"EXACT_OPUS_QUOTE","securityDisclosureRef":{"label":"Full Opus scope and assumptions","document":"SECURITY-COPY.json","pointer":"/cards/5","pointerType":"json-pointer"}},{"id":"partial-evidence","question":"Is a remaining payment duty a missing proof?","answer":"Financial partial progress (a duty remaining) and a missing required proof are separate states with separate controls. The invoice worksheet never changes Web completeness, and no proof badge appears in the worksheet.","sourceRefs":[{"label":"Opus role06 wording","document":"SECURITY-COPY.json","pointer":"/diagramRules/8/rule","pointerType":"json-pointer"}],"wording":"EXACT_OPUS_QUOTE","securityDisclosureRef":{"label":"Full Opus scope and assumptions","document":"SECURITY-COPY.json","pointer":"/cards/5","pointerType":"json-pointer"}},{"id":"partial-range","question":"Why does the payment stop at 99?","answer":"Slider domain: integers 1 to 99 inclusive. S12 partial requires amount > 0 and amount < opening, and opening is 100.","sourceRefs":[{"label":"Opus role06 wording","document":"SECURITY-COPY.json","pointer":"/invoiceRules/1/rule","pointerType":"json-pointer"}],"wording":"EXACT_OPUS_QUOTE"},{"id":"proposed-funding","question":"Can S12 fund the escrow with 112?","answer":"PROPOSED full-backing view: funding 112, cash = 112 − amount − 2, which equals total duties after one partial. Label it PROPOSED application policy. S12 open funds exactly face + reserve and has no extra transfer, so 112 is not an S12 action, and the source enforces only non-negative custody, not backing.","sourceRefs":[{"label":"Opus role06 wording","document":"SECURITY-COPY.json","pointer":"/invoiceRules/4/rule","pointerType":"json-pointer"}],"wording":"EXACT_OPUS_QUOTE","securityDisclosureRef":{"label":"Full Opus scope and assumptions","document":"SECURITY-COPY.json","pointer":"/cards/7","pointerType":"json-pointer"}},{"id":"reserve-duty","question":"Does finishing the invoice return the reserve?","answer":"Reserve: S12 has no reserve-duty-return action, so reserve duty 10 remains. If finish is mentioned, the source path leaves cash 8 against reserve duty 10 (the proposed 112 path leaves 10 against 10), and the nonzero reserve duty blocks V2 Close either way. Never show closed, complete or reserve returned.","sourceRefs":[{"label":"Opus role06 wording","document":"SECURITY-COPY.json","pointer":"/invoiceRules/7/rule","pointerType":"json-pointer"}],"wording":"EXACT_OPUS_QUOTE","securityDisclosureRef":{"label":"Full Opus scope and assumptions","document":"SECURITY-COPY.json","pointer":"/cards/7","pointerType":"json-pointer"}},{"id":"where-checked","question":"Where would proofs be checked?","answer":"Issuance is the reviewed V2 route. Verification at consumption is PROPOSED and needs a new redeemer and in-settlement pairings.","sourceRefs":[{"label":"Opus role06 wording","document":"SECURITY-COPY.json","pointer":"/glossaryClarifications/4/clarification","pointerType":"json-pointer"}],"wording":"EXACT_OPUS_QUOTE","securityDisclosureRef":{"label":"Full Opus scope and assumptions","document":"SECURITY-COPY.json","pointer":"/cards/4","pointerType":"json-pointer"}},{"id":"permissionless","question":"Do developers need Moriarty approval?","answer":"Supported public tooling is permissionless. Maintainer, council, campaign and registry approval are not developer prerequisites. Asset-owner authorization and objective target validity still apply.","sourceRefs":[{"label":"Permissionless product boundary","document":"CONCEPT.md","pointer":"Developer experience and ecosystem delivery","pointerType":"markdown-heading"}]},{"id":"sdk","question":"Can I use these as SDK instructions?","answer":"These are proposed learning stages, not shipping SDK methods or runnable commands. Follow a target-specific capability guide and stop at unsupported boundaries.","sourceRefs":[{"label":"Proposed developer interfaces","document":"DEVELOPER-EXPERIENCE.md","pointer":"Current source commands and future ergonomics","pointerType":"markdown-heading"}]}],"evidence":[{"id":"current-record","title":"Current maturity","body":"Moriarty Webs is a concept under review. Supplied records include one current-Rust invoice-partial Groth16 roundtrip (S07) and seven finite local compiled-C2-wrapper cases (S08: one acceptance and six mutation rejections), with a single-party test setup whose operator can forge proofs and caller-fabricated proof contexts. No on-chain transaction is recorded. Only a conditional lowering argument is adopted. V2 consumer descriptions on this page are Opus readings of unadopted, uncompiled source, not observed behavior or exact-source correspondence to those runs. General Web integration, genuine intent authorization, onchain settlement and all-family compatibility remain unestablished in this packet. Local VM cost is not ledger cost. Every interactive example on this page is an illustration.","stage":"RECORDED","limitations":["Recorded observation in a supplied S07/S08 record; local and scoped, not ledger acceptance."],"sourceRefs":[{"label":"Opus maturity wording","document":"SECURITY-COPY.json","pointer":"/maturity/body","pointerType":"json-pointer"},{"label":"S07: one current-Rust invoice-partial roundtrip","document":"S07-K6-ACTUAL-ADOPTION.json","pointer":"/scope","pointerType":"json-pointer"},{"label":"S08: seven finite local compiled-wrapper cases","document":"S08-K10-ACTUAL-ADOPTION.json","pointer":"/predicate","pointerType":"json-pointer"},{"label":"S08: result limitations","document":"S08-K10-ACTUAL-ADOPTION.json","pointer":"/scopeLimitations","pointerType":"json-pointer"}],"wording":"EXACT_OPUS_QUOTE"},{"id":"conditional-t1","title":"Conditional source argument only","body":"A01 adopts only conditional T1: the all-role conjunction equals whole-action Core over one decoded body, modulo premise G including output well-formedness, a covering WebPlan, whole-action compilation first and the same field.","stage":"CONDITIONAL_ARGUMENT","limitations":["There is no implementation, no T2, no T3 and no full WP migration (F07–F10); builder reliance is not permitted."],"sourceRefs":[{"label":"Opus: conditional T1 and explicit exclusions","document":"SECURITY-COPY.json","pointer":"/cards/5/assumptions/2","pointerType":"json-pointer"}],"wording":"EXACT_OPUS_QUOTE_SPLIT_WITHOUT_CHANGE"}],"securityScope":{"id":"v2-source-scope","body":"Every V2 consumer or claim-policy description here is an Opus source-level reading of unadopted, uncompiled Aiken/Rust files. It is not observed compiled behavior and not exact-source evidence for the recorded S07 roundtrip or the S08 compiled C2-wrapper (K10) runs: no supplied record pins those binaries or compiled bytes to the reviewed source. The four-piece settle implementation and byte/type helpers were not supplied to the reviewer; four-piece reuse rests on S09 and source comments.","attribution":"Opus role06; exact wording, not a security judgment by role08.","sourceRefs":[{"label":"Opus source-reading caveat","document":"SECURITY-COPY.json","pointer":"/statusScope/sourceReadingCaveat","pointerType":"json-pointer"}],"displayRule":"Keep this scope adjacent to V2 descriptions or one accessible disclosure away."},"nextSteps":["Explore the invoice worksheet.","Compare the five proposed applications.","Open the evidence and its limitations."],"broaderScope":{"body":"One example does not establish compatibility. The wider language and evidence requirements remain open.","retainedFamilies":["S01","S02","S03","S04","escrow-time","interval-boundary","joint-role","oracle-observation","bilateral atomic escrow swap","constant-product exact rounding/slippage with serialized pool continuation","order execution versus timeout cancellation","two-order batch exact input coverage and injective output allocation / same-output double satisfaction","F01-origination","F02-interest-period","F03-partial-repayment-residual-liability","F04-fee-gross-net-policy","AP01","AP02","AP03","AP04"],"familiesStatus":"Requirements, not supported ports.","actus":"277 fixtures, 18 executable types, 32 taxonomy dispositions and DS01–07.","defi":"72 rows, DA01–24, held-outs, intent cases and regressions.","furtherTracks":["General financial obligations","Universal property certificates","Authenticated history","Recursion","Private handoff","Bounded multiparent PCD"],"target":"Midnight ZKIRv3 and native proof infrastructure remain the broader target; Cardano is an additional experiment.","qualification":"Preview-first and later Preprod end-to-end acceptance remain required for claimed targets.","catalogue":"235 pre-dedup review identifiers, not proven properties. Original 42/52 lists remain unrecovered. Required setup count is unknown.","sourceRefs":[{"label":"Exact retained family IDs","document":"S02-PATTERN-INVENTORY.json","pointer":"","pointerType":"json-pointer"},{"label":"Retained financial and composition scope","document":"S07-K6-ACTUAL-ADOPTION.json","pointer":"/allRequiredScopeRetained","pointerType":"json-pointer"},{"label":"Broader target and provenance boundaries","document":"CONCEPT.md","pointer":"Evidence appendix and retained breadth","pointerType":"markdown-heading"}]},"worksheetConstraints":{"status":"ILLUSTRATION_ONLY","sourceAmountRange":[1,99],"amount0":"SOURCE_PARTIAL_REFUSED","amount100":"SOURCE_PARTIAL_REFUSED","funding110":"S12 source reading, not executed.","funding112":"PROPOSED application policy; not reachable with S12 as written.","laterPartialFees":"Funding 112 backs one partial only. Each further partial charges escrow another 2 that no duty reflects, so later fees create further gaps unless a further rule and funding are adopted.","sourceRefs":[{"label":"Literal partial predicate and fixture correction","document":"SOURCE-PRECONDITION-CLARIFICATION.json","pointer":"/worksheetSourceValidAmountRange","pointerType":"json-pointer"},{"label":"112 is not an S12 action","document":"SECURITY-COPY.json","pointer":"/invoiceRules/4","pointerType":"json-pointer"},{"label":"Further fees","document":"SECURITY-COPY.json","pointer":"/invoiceRules/5","pointerType":"json-pointer"}]}},"v2":{"format":"moriarty-webs-v2-copy/1","author":"Opus 5.5 HIGH (requested) security and cryptographic copy author for the v2 Webs and Strands page. Authored copy, not a review of the integrated page; fresh exact-page Muse and Opus reviews follow.","presentation":"User direction 2026-10-16 (don't ai slop hedge, the html explains the fully implemented concept. We will finish this implementation) supersedes historical requirements for pervasive PROPOSED/illustrative/future labels, exact diagram strings and adjacent per-paragraph caveats within this page. The main object explains the complete product design in present tense. Actual implementation status, trust assumptions and evidence appear once, in the technical object.","templateVariables":{"amount":"Seller payment; integer 1 to 99 inclusive; default 35","fee":"Partial-payment program fee; constant 2","debit":"Gross escrow debit = amount + fee","openingCash":"Escrow opening cash; main default 112 (fee-funded design); 110 only in the technical source comparison","cash":"Escrow cash after the payment = openingCash − amount − fee","invoiceDuty":"100 − amount","reserveDuty":"Constant 10","duties":"invoiceDuty + reserveDuty = 110 − amount"},"main":{"heroBody":"Moriarty is a language for financial contracts. Webs let a Cardano application attach proofs to a transaction, so its validator can check that the contract’s rules were followed.","explorerIntro":"An invoice is one use for a Web. Choose another application to see the conditions its contract checks. Select a condition to read the claim and where its inputs come from; remove proof coverage for it to see why an incomplete set cannot settle.","proofRoleExplanation":"A condition is something the contract requires, such as a correct fee calculation. A Strand contains a Groth16 proof, its public claim and the information tying it to this transaction. One proof can cover several conditions. The settlement validator rebuilds the claim from the transaction and checks that it matches the claim whose proof was verified. An external price or delivery record still needs an authenticated source and a freshness rule.","definitions":[{"term":"Strand","body":"A Strand carries one Groth16 proof, the claim it proves and the information that ties it to a particular transaction. For an invoice, its claim might cover the payment calculation and the unpaid balance."},{"term":"Web","body":"A Web collects the Strands a contract requires for a transaction. The contract sets those requirements. Settlement needs every required condition covered by a matching Strand."},{"term":"Claim specification","body":"A claim specification describes a kind of claim: what it asserts, which inputs it uses and which compiled rules it must satisfy. It can be reused across transactions; a proof for an old state cannot simply be reused for a changed one."}],"assemblyComplete":"All required conditions are covered. The Web can be submitted for settlement; the validator still checks it against the actual transaction.","assemblyIncomplete":"A required condition has no proof coverage. This Web cannot authorize settlement. Any proof claims already issued remain locked until settlement or a claimant-signed reclaim after expiry.","worksheet":{"title":"Choose how much to pay the seller","intro":"The invoice is for 100 CASH, with a separate reserve obligation of 10 CASH. The escrow—the account holding the funds—starts with {openingCash} CASH. Each partial payment costs a 2 CASH program fee.","modeLabel":"112 CASH · fee included","modeBody":"The extra 2 CASH funds the fee for this payment. Afterward, the remaining cash covers both the unpaid invoice and the reserve.","amountLabel":"Payment to the seller","amountRangeNote":"Choose 1–99 CASH for a partial payment. Paying the full invoice uses a separate action.","rows":{"sellerReceives":"Seller receives {amount} CASH","programFee":"Program fee to the fee recipient {fee} CASH","grossDebit":"Gross escrow debit {debit} CASH","escrowCash":"Escrow cash after payment {cash} CASH","invoiceDuty":"Invoice duty {invoiceDuty} CASH","reserveDuty":"Reserve duty {reserveDuty} CASH","totalDuties":"Total duties {duties} CASH"},"backingLine":"The remaining {cash} CASH covers {duties} CASH still owed.","feeRule":"The {amount} CASH payment reduces the invoice balance. The separate {fee} CASH fee does not.","oneStepNote":"This example funds one partial payment. Another partial payment would need another {fee} CASH fee allowance.","reserveNote":"The {reserveDuty} CASH reserve obligation remains.","proofSeparation":"A partial payment still needs all of its proofs. An unpaid balance does not make the Web incomplete.","assetNote":"CASH is an example unit in the invoice program.","networkFeeNote":"Cardano network fees, collateral and the minimum ADA held in proof-claim outputs are separate; this worksheet does not estimate them."},"lifecycleTitle":"How a Web reaches settlement","lifecycleIntro":"The example uses an issuance route: proofs are checked in earlier transactions, then their on-chain claim outputs are consumed together at settlement. Creating those outputs does not execute the invoice payment.","lifecycleSteps":[{"id":"authorize","title":"The owner signs limits","body":"Before proving, the owner signs an intent that limits what may leave the account and what each recipient must receive. It also identifies the permitted fees, validity window and proof relation. A one-time nonce prevents the same authorization being used again."},{"id":"prove","title":"A prover builds the Web","body":"The prover runs the financial program and produces the required Strands. Each claim is tied to the state being spent, its sequence number, the settlement script, the catalogue of permitted claims and verification keys, signed intents and claimant. One execution proof can cover several required conditions."},{"id":"issue","title":"Proof claims go on-chain","body":"An Issue transaction verifies one Groth16 proof and creates a claim output holding minimum ADA. This output records checked evidence for later settlement. It does not pay the seller or mint the application’s asset."},{"id":"settle","title":"The transaction settles","body":"Settlement consumes every required claim output and burns its claim token. The validator rebuilds the claims from the transaction, checks the signed limits and requires the claimants’ signatures. The application then checks inclusion and its required finality, and reads back the payments and remaining obligations."}],"faqs":[{"id":"claim-vs-asset","question":"Does a proof claim mint my application’s token?","answer":"No. Issuing a proof claim creates an output that records checked evidence. Settlement consumes it and burns the claim token. Your application’s token has its own minting policy, which controls supply, recipients and any issuance cap.","technicalCardId":"issuance-route"},{"id":"permission","question":"Do I need permission from Moriarty to build an application?","answer":"No. Developers can write, compile, prove and deploy programs in the supported language without approval from Moriarty maintainers. The application still has to follow the ledger’s rules and respect the authorization of anyone whose assets it moves. A contract may also define access rules for its own users.","technicalCardId":"scope-and-permission"},{"id":"owner-approval","question":"What does the owner authorize?","answer":"The owner signs an intent before proving. It defines per-asset limits on gross debits, fees and new liabilities, as well as the minimum net amount each beneficiary must receive. Its validity window, one-time nonce and exact proof relation limit where and when it can be used. Network fees are shown separately.","technicalCardId":"effects-authority"},{"id":"signers","question":"Who signs the settlement transaction?","answer":"Each proof-claim output names a claimant. Consuming it at settlement requires that claimant’s signature and burns the claim token. The validator also checks the prior signed intents of the accounts being debited. Those owner authorizations and the later claimant signatures serve different purposes.","technicalCardId":"signer-boundary"},{"id":"readback","question":"How do I know the payment happened?","answer":"Check that the transaction was included, wait for the finality your application requires, then read back the actual outputs and remaining obligations. Reconcile those amounts with the owner’s signed intent. A timeout leaves the outcome unknown; it does not establish that the payment failed.","technicalCardId":"readback-boundary"},{"id":"partial-vs-missing","question":"Can a partial payment have a complete Web?","answer":"Yes. Completeness means every required condition for this payment has proof coverage. It does not mean the entire invoice has been paid. The unpaid invoice balance and reserve obligation remain in the next state.","technicalCardId":"web-completeness"},{"id":"unused-claims","question":"What if the Web never settles?","answer":"The issued claim outputs keep holding their minimum ADA. Their claimant can reclaim them after the chosen expiry by signing a reclaim transaction. If a catalogue account advances its sequence through revocation, or a competing settlement spends the same state, the old claims can no longer settle and must wait for expiry.","technicalCardId":"issuance-route"},{"id":"cost","question":"Does splitting a Web into more Strands reduce the cost?","answer":"Each Strand needs its own Issue transaction on this route, and its claim output holds minimum ADA until settlement or reclaim. A single execution proof covering several conditions may be simpler. Comparing costs requires measuring complete transactions on the target network; splitting alone does not establish a saving.","technicalCardId":"page-scope"}],"caseCopy":[{"id":"invoice","summary":"Pay {amount} CASH toward an invoice held in escrow. The seller receives the payment and the fee recipient receives a separate {fee} CASH. The unpaid invoice balance and reserve obligation remain in the next state.","roles":[{"id":"invoice-payment","label":"Payment calculation","claim":"The seller receives the chosen partial payment.","statement":"Opening invoice duty 100 CASH; payment {amount} CASH; escrow debit for payment and fee {debit} CASH.","authentication":"The opening duty comes from the escrow state this transition spends, and CASH is the asset the invoice program declares."},{"id":"invoice-fee","label":"Separate program fee","claim":"The escrow pays a separate {fee} CASH fee, which does not reduce the invoice balance.","statement":"Escrow pays the seller {amount} CASH and the fee recipient {fee} CASH; the fee does not reduce invoice duty.","authentication":"The consuming validator checks the actual seller and fee-recipient outputs against these amounts."},{"id":"invoice-duty","label":"Remaining obligations","claim":"The next state records the unpaid invoice balance and the unchanged reserve obligation.","statement":"Invoice 100 − {amount} = {invoiceDuty} CASH; reserve duty stays {reserveDuty} CASH; total duties {duties} CASH; escrow keeps {cash} CASH.","authentication":"Duties carry over from the spent escrow state and change only through recorded accruals and discharges."}]},{"id":"dex","summary":"Exchange assets through a liquidity pool, combining two orders in one transaction. Each owner sets a minimum amount to receive. The contract must check both the pool calculation and what each owner actually receives.","roles":[{"id":"dex-orders","label":"Each owner’s result","claim":"Each owner receives at least the minimum amount they agreed to.","statement":"Two orders each offer 100 A and require at least 90 B net; each owner receives 92 B.","authentication":"Asset identities and each owner’s limits come from the owners’ signed intents; the validator checks the actual outputs."},{"id":"dex-pool","label":"Pool update","claim":"The updated pool reserves follow the pool’s rules for fees and rounding.","statement":"The reserve update, fee rule and exact rounding agree with both orders.","authentication":"Pool reserves come from the pool state being spent; any outside price carries its named source and freshness limit."},{"id":"dex-batch","label":"Combined order accounting","claim":"The combined transaction accounts for both orders without counting one payment toward two orders.","statement":"The amounts used for each owner’s result are the amounts in the batch accounting, and no payment counts toward two orders.","authentication":"The consuming validator allocates actual outputs under its profile’s rule: one output per order, or an exact partition that admits combined payments with every order allocated once."}]},{"id":"issuance","summary":"Create 300 units of an application token under a cap of 1,000, then distribute them to two recipients. The cap leaves room for up to 700 more units under the minting policy; that capacity is not an amount already owed.","roles":[{"id":"issuance-supply","label":"Supply limit","claim":"The number of tokens issued stays within the cap.","statement":"First issue: 300 units under a cap of 1,000; unissued capacity is 700.","authentication":"The application asset’s own minting policy enforces the cap and continues the supply state from its predecessor."},{"id":"issuance-distribution","label":"Recipient amounts","claim":"The amounts sent to the recipients add up to the number of tokens issued.","statement":"Recipient one receives 180 units and recipient two receives 120 units; 180 + 120 = 300.","authentication":"The minting policy and the consuming validator check the actual mint and recipient outputs."},{"id":"issuance-duties","label":"Separate token obligations","claim":"Issuing tokens creates a servicing obligation only if the program defines one.","statement":"The remaining 700 units of capacity are not debt. A redemption promise is a duty only when the program defines it.","authentication":"A servicing duty names its parties, its exact asset and its creation and discharge rules; eligibility documents keep their issuer and freshness limits."}]},{"id":"loan","summary":"A debt of 100 CASH grows by 5 CASH for the period. A 35 CASH repayment reduces it to 70 CASH. The borrower also pays a 2 CASH fee, which does not repay any debt.","roles":[{"id":"loan-accrual","label":"Updated debt","claim":"The debt increases by the period’s accrued amount.","statement":"Debt 100 CASH plus accrual 5 CASH gives a duty of 105 CASH.","authentication":"The loan agreement fixes period, rate and rounding; a variable rate carries its named observation source and freshness rule."},{"id":"loan-payment","label":"Repayment and fee","claim":"The payer funds the debt repayment and its separate fee.","statement":"Debt payment 35 CASH plus separate fee 2 CASH gives payer gross debit 37 CASH.","authentication":"Payer, lender, fee recipient and asset match the payer’s signed intent and the transaction’s actual outputs."},{"id":"loan-duty","label":"Remaining debt","claim":"Only the repayment reduces the debt; the fee is accounted for separately.","statement":"Duty 105 − repayment 35 = 70 CASH remaining; the 2 CASH fee is not repayment.","authentication":"The continuing debt carries over from the predecessor state through explicit accrual and discharge entries; collateral release follows its own policy."}]},{"id":"treasury","summary":"Allocate funds to two beneficiaries while paying a separate fee. The transaction also creates reserve and rebate obligations, so the treasury must retain enough of each asset to cover them.","roles":[{"id":"treasury-quotient","label":"Division and remainder","claim":"The allocation uses the correct quotient and remainder when dividing 23 by 3.","statement":"Dividing 23 by 3 gives 7 with a remainder of 2: 23 = 3 × 7 + 2, and the remainder is less than 3. The program call is allocate(true,23,7,2), where true supplies the approval flag.","authentication":"The allocation inputs, including approved=true, sit inside the intent the debited treasury account signs; a separate governance approver must itself be a required signer, an advancer or an authenticated input."},{"id":"treasury-payments","label":"Beneficiary payments","claim":"The beneficiary payments and fee match the allocation calculation.","statement":"Alpha receives 14 A and 4 B; beta receives 7 A; fees receive 2 A. Total custody debit is 23 A and 4 B.","authentication":"Treasury custody, the identities of A and B and the beneficiary outputs are checked by the consuming validator."},{"id":"treasury-reserve","label":"Funds retained for obligations","claim":"The treasury keeps enough of assets A and B to cover the new reserve and rebate obligations.","statement":"Allocation creates a reserve duty of 2 A and a rebate duty of 6 B. The treasury holds 25 A and 10 B before the step and keeps 2 A and 6 B against those duties.","authentication":"Opening custody comes from the treasury state being spent; the consumer enforces that custody covers the duties."}]}],"heroExample":"For an invoice payment, that means checking how much reaches the seller and how much is still owed after the fee. The example below follows those amounts through a single payment."},"technical":{"title":"Implementation status and evidence","summary":"The page above describes Moriarty Webs as designed. This section records where the implementation stands. Supplied records include one current-Rust invoice-partial Groth16 roundtrip (S07) and seven local compiled-C2-wrapper cases (S08: one acceptance and six mutation rejections), with a single-party test setup whose operator can forge proofs and caller-fabricated proof contexts. No on-chain transaction is recorded. Descriptions of the consumer, claim policy, owner intents, duties and Close are Opus readings of unadopted, uncompiled V2 Aiken/Rust source, not observed behavior, and no supplied record pins the S07/S08 binaries or compiled bytes to that source. Of the composition argument, only conditional T1 is adopted; T2, T3 (compiled circuit/VK/BLAKE2b gadget correspondence) and the full WP migration are open. Not yet built: the all-or-nothing Web consumer and required-set enforcement, partial-step enforcement, real-context proofs, genuine intent signatures, the CLI/SDK and transaction build/submit/observe interfaces, a wallet signing display, the funding and backing actions behind the 112 CASH and 25 A / 10 B designs, reserve-duty discharge, batch issuance and any production setup ceremony. S08’s local VM figures are not ledger cost, and no cost, latency or portability advantage is measured or claimed.","sourceVersusDesign":"Invoice. The worksheet uses the fee-funded design: escrow opens with 112 CASH. The executed source differs. S12 open(90,10) funds escrow with exactly 110 CASH (invoice duty 100 = 90 + 10, reserve duty 10) and charges the buyer a separate 3 CASH opening fee, a gross debit of 113. On that source path one partial of amount a debits a + 2 and leaves 110 − a − 2 against duties of 110 − a: always 2 short (at 35: debit 37, cash 73 against duties 65 + 10 = 75). S12 checks only non-negative custody, not backing, and has no extra-funding transfer, so 112 needs a program change and a consumer-enforced backing policy. The buyer’s opening debit under the 112 design is not specified by any source. 112 backs one partial only; each further partial charges escrow another 2 that no duty reflects, so a sequence of partials needs a further funding rule. S12 has no reserve-duty-return action: after finish the source path leaves 8 against reserve duty 10 and the 112 path leaves 10 against 10, and the nonzero reserve duty blocks V2 Close on either path; discharge or migration needs a program change. Treasury. S13 allocate(true,23,7,2) needs only 23 A and 4 B for non-negative custody; holding 25 A and 10 B so that 2 A and 6 B remain is a full-backing policy a consumer must enforce. units is checked only arithmetically (23 = 3×7 + 2) and is never debited. rebate(amount) can discharge the B rebate; S13 has no action discharging allocation_reserve, so Close stays blocked. DEX, issuance and loan amounts are worked examples with no supplied source program.","networkFeeNote":"Cardano network fees, collateral, the min-ADA held by each proof-claim output from issuance until settlement or claimant-signed post-expiry reclaim, and the V2 thread reserve (lovelace returned on Close) are separate from the CASH amounts in the worksheet. None is estimated on this page. A program-asset fee is neither debt repayment nor a network fee, and a reserve duty is a program liability, not the thread reserve.","signerBoundary":"What the reviewed V2 source fixes (uncompiled-source reading): owners sign intents before proving; each intent signs per-asset gross_cap, fee_cap, net_floor and liability_cap for a beneficiary, a validity window, nonce = seq and the target predicate, VK and shape digests. Each Issue transaction carries one proof, checked by the C2 claim policy, and mints one claim output. The settlement transaction requires the signature of each claimant whose claim output it consumes and burns that output’s token. Unused claims are reclaimed with the claimant’s signature after the claimant-chosen expiry. A supplied approved=true is consented to by the effect-derived required signers, and by a listed advancer if any; that is the debited account’s consent, not a separate governance decision. The claimant is prover-chosen bytes bound at consumption, not an authenticated prover identity. Open: no transaction-by-transaction signer matrix (intent authorization, each Issue, settlement, reclaim) is published; the supplied material does not state who must sign an Issue transaction; no genuine intent signature is observed in the supplied proof records; no wallet signing display exists.","readbackBoundary":"Design: after submission the application separates inclusion, required finality and complete readback of outputs and remaining duties, and reconciles actual per-asset effects against the signed intents; a timeout leaves observation unknown and is not evidence of nonexecution. Status: no transaction build, submit or observe interface exists; no on-chain transaction is recorded; the acceptance and readback signal for a supported target still has to be defined and demonstrated, Preview first and later Preprod.","cardsIntro":"The cards below keep the reviewed role06 wording exactly. Labels such as PROPOSED, OPEN, PREMISE and V2 SOURCE READING describe the implementation status of the design explained above.","trustNotes":[{"id":"proof-meaning","title":"What a verifying proof shows","body":"Under an honest setup and Groth16 knowledge soundness, a verifying proof shows the prover knew a witness satisfying the compiled constraints for its two public inputs. Linking those inputs to statement bytes additionally rests on the in-circuit BLAKE2b gadget and on BLAKE2b collision resistance. It never authenticates those bytes. A proof over supplied values does not make them an authenticated ledger fact or a true outside observation. A concrete execution proof, a universal property certificate and an authenticated ledger claim are different assertions; the latter two are not established Strand kinds.","assumptions":["The current class-0 test setup is forgeable by its operator, so no production security claim follows.","Whether the compiled constraints and verifying key match your source is OPEN (T3: compiled circuit/VK and BLAKE2b gadget correspondence).","Outside facts keep their named issuer, predicate, authentication path and freshness limits.","Reference-input reads are PROPOSED: the validator must read the datum itself and authenticate its origin. PREMISE: being unspent at application shows presence only, with no exclusivity or history. A validity interval neither dates nor authenticates a datum.","SEC06-P06 PROPOSED; still needs real-context stale-sequence and expired-attestation cases, changed-reference-datum and forged-origin negatives, N5/N7–N9 at V, and a separately scoped real-context proof."],"evidenceStage":"Assumption-bound Groth16 meaning; T3 OPEN; reference-input path PROPOSED","sourceRefs":["ERR-16","ERR-17","ERR-01","ERR-11","CL-27","CL-45","CL-49","S07 /limitations/universalPropertyCertificate","S07 /limitations/class0ControllerCanForge","SEC06-P06 PROPOSED"]},{"id":"recorded-evidence","title":"What has actually been recorded","body":"Evidence layers (DEMO ONLY): H ordinary host tests captured (K4H, S04; fresh actual-result review pending) | N 1 local roundtrip (S07) | V 1 local C2 wrapper, 7 cases (S08) | P none. One genuine proof was accepted by a compiled C2 wrapper in a local VM with fabricated context (S08); no on-chain transaction is recorded. S08 /cases/0 records cpu 2222340191 and mem 205312 for the whole compiled C2 Issue evaluation, including its pairing; it is neither an isolated verification cost nor ledger ExUnits, fee or transaction fit. Every recorded proof context in the supplied sources is caller-fabricated (S07, S08).","assumptions":["No supplied record pins the recorded runs to the later V2 source bytes; V2 readings elsewhere on this page are not evidence about these runs.","Named runtime guards behind the six S08 rejections were not instrumented.","All 20 retained S02 families are requirements, not supported ports. The 235 catalogue IDs are pre-dedup review identifiers, not accepted or proven properties; the original 42/52 lists remain unrecovered.","Moriarty's broader target remains Midnight ZKIRv3 and native proof infrastructure; Cardano is an additional experiment and does not qualify Midnight. Preview-first and later Preprod end-to-end acceptance remain required for claimed targets.","SEC06-P08 PROPOSED (developer trust report); still needs a DEMO ONLY S07/S08 report with these qualified layers, fresh nonsecurity wording review and fresh security review. No new evidence is inferred from this layer wording."],"evidenceStage":"RECORDED (local, scoped)","sourceRefs":["ERR-18","ERR-17","ERR-05","ERR-19","CL-11","CL-12","CL-13","CL-14","CL-31","CL-32","CL-33","CL-34","S07 /scope","S08 /cases","S08 /scopeLimitations","SEC06-P08 PROPOSED"]},{"id":"setup-keys","title":"Setup and keys","body":"The current test setup can be forged by its operator; whether the verifying key matches the compiled relation is OPEN. Groth16 needs a separate setup per distinct compiled circuit (R1CS matrices), not per logical predicate. In the current Rust source the predicate digest is a circuit constant, so each distinct relation identity is a distinct circuit. The required total setup count is unknown; predicate, catalogue and family counts do not determine it. No production ceremony is recorded in the supplied sources, and none is proposed here.","assumptions":["A future universal circuit or Midnight's universal PLONK/KZG SRS would change the comparison (PROPOSED or target-specific).","V2 SOURCE READING: catalogue VK digests are fixed at genesis and carried unchanged, and Close requires zero balances and duties, so any key change needs a PROPOSED key migration action that carries residual duties.","Intent revocation (Revoke) advances seq; it is not key migration.","SEC06-P07 PROPOSED; still needs an old-VK-refused-after-migration negative, a setup-class report and independent review of any future ceremony."],"evidenceStage":"RECORDED limitation (forgeable test setup); V2 SOURCE READING; total setup count UNKNOWN","sourceRefs":["ERR-14","ERR-28","ERR-17","CL-14","CL-32","CL-43","SEC06-P07 PROPOSED"]},{"id":"binding-chain","title":"What ties a Strand to its step","body":"V2 SOURCE READING: tag, family, predicate_digest, shape_digest and body_len are circuit constants; the context fields vk_digest through intents_digest (bytes 112..339, including claimant_vkh) are free booleanized witness bits that are hashed, not interpreted. The consumer, not the circuit, ties those fields to the transaction: the settling validator rebuilds each statement from the transaction and the spent thread state, so a claim made for another thread step, catalogue, claimant or intent set does not match. Claimant binding happens at consumption, not at issuance: C2 Issue does not compare the claimant with the statement, so a mismatched envelope can be minted but cannot settle. This source-level binding is separate from T3, which stays OPEN.","assumptions":["The class-0 setup operator can forge a proof for any digest and any claimant. The claimant is prover-chosen bytes, not an authenticated prover identity.","Builders select claim inputs; the ledger spends each claim once (PREMISE). A copied proof can be re-issued with the same claimant and a new expiry; only that claimant can release it.","Catalogue program_hash and source_hash are visible, covered by catalogue_digest and signed in every owner intent. No on-chain check links them to predicate_digest or vk_digest: that link is a CATALOGUE-AUTHOR ASSERTION and VK-to-matrix is OPEN. Exact predicate-digest field coverage depends on identity.rs, which the reviewer did not read.","The statement's network byte is declared by the catalogue and not compared with the executing ledger; separation across networks rests on outref uniqueness (PREMISE).","SEC06-P03 and SEC06-P04 PROPOSED; still need competing-claimant and same-claimant duplicate negatives, second-party source→relation→predicate reproduction, refusal-list coverage, N5/N7–N9, a T3 method, reviewed T2/T3 and F07–F10, a non-test setup, and cross-network replay analysis if the network byte is relied on."],"evidenceStage":"V2 SOURCE READING (unadopted, uncompiled); T3 OPEN","sourceRefs":["ERR-02","ERR-03","ERR-13","ERR-07","ERR-16","CL-36","CL-39","CL-40","CL-45","CL-46","SEC06-P03 PROPOSED","SEC06-P04 PROPOSED"]},{"id":"issuance-route","title":"Where proofs are checked: the issuance route","body":"In the reviewed V2 source, each proof is checked when its claim is issued: the C2 claim policy checks a Groth16 proof and mints one claim output per Issue transaction. Claims must exist before settlement. The settlement transaction consumes them, recomputes each statement and compares it with the digest checked at issuance; it does not verify the proof again. Each consumed claim needs its claimant's signature on the settlement transaction and the burn of its token. Structurally, n required Strands mean n Issue transactions plus one settlement, excluding one-time genesis and any post-expiry reclaim. These counts are readings of uncompiled source, not measurements.","assumptions":["Each claim output locks min-ADA from issuance until settlement or claimant-signed reclaim after the claimant-chosen expiry. Several envelopes can exist per statement digest.","Safe but not live: any catalogue account may Revoke (advancing seq), and a competing settlement may spend the outref, orphaning issued claims and locking their value until expiry. Earlier reclaim is PROPOSED (OPEN).","Batch issuance needs a C2 change (PROPOSED). Proof-at-consumption is a different PROPOSED design needing a new redeemer and in-settlement pairings, with size and budget unmeasured. Mempool chaining is a node premise outside the sources.","V2 source already refuses two claims for one consumed outref; S09's role-level refusal is an unadopted proposal and not deduplication permission.","Owner intents are signed before proving; claimant settlement signatures are a later, distinct event. Proof collection or claim-output creation executes no effect.","Still needs orphan-after-Revoke, competing-settlement and post-expiry reclaim cases, and a qualified complete-path measurement of both verification placements."],"evidenceStage":"V2 SOURCE READING (structural); not observed onchain","sourceRefs":["ERR-04","ERR-06","ERR-07","ERR-12","ERR-25","ERR-26","ERR-27","CL-02","CL-30","CL-47","CL-50","SEC06-P04 PROPOSED"]},{"id":"web-completeness","title":"Complete and incomplete Webs","body":"PROPOSED: a Web settles only when every Strand its contract requires is present. An incomplete Web has no authority over thread effects. On the issuance route its already-issued claim outputs exist on-chain, lock value, and remain releasable only by their claimant. In the first proposed supported profile, the consumer refuses universal property certificates, authenticated-history claims, private handoff, recursive parents and multiparent joins as UNSUPPORTED; they remain required Web tracks, each needing its own trust path and evidence before any profile accepts it.","assumptions":["All-or-nothing acceptance and a consumer-derived required set are PROPOSED; no Web consumer, second genuine relation or general cardinality exists in this packet.","Depicting several Strand roles does not establish separate relations or producers. A whole-execution proof may cover several claim specifications; naming source expressions fee or liability does not create independent proof producers.","A01 adopts only conditional T1: the all-role conjunction equals whole-action Core over one decoded body, modulo premise G including output well-formedness, a covering WebPlan, whole-action compilation first and the same field. There is no implementation, no T2, no T3 and no full WP migration (F07–F10); builder reliance is not permitted.","OPEN: whether a consumer can enforce a fully authorized partial financial step that carries every proof that step requires, distinct from a missing proof.","SEC06-P01 and SEC06-P02 PROPOSED; still need N1/N12 and unknown-class refusal, a report-level test that an incomplete set never renders as accepted, N1–N4/N6/N11/N14 at H/V, a wrong-action-index case, a required-set display, separate future-profile designs and fresh security review of reused V2 mechanisms."],"evidenceStage":"PROPOSED; CONDITIONAL_ARGUMENT for T1 only","sourceRefs":["ERR-01","ERR-15","ERR-10","ERR-20","ERR-27","CL-03","CL-15","CL-16","CL-17","CL-21","CL-37","CL-38","SEC06-P01 PROPOSED","SEC06-P02 PROPOSED"]},{"id":"effects-authority","title":"Effects, allocation and authority","body":"V2 SOURCE READING: owner intents sign per-asset gross_cap, fee_cap, net_floor and liability_cap for a beneficiary, a validity window, nonce = seq and the target predicate, VK and shape digests. Duties change only through recorded accruals and discharges, and a thread with a nonzero duty cannot close. The thread datum carries the complete custody snapshot, which custody must equal exactly. Actual output allocation is the consuming validator's job; a proof can attest arithmetic only over values the validator recomputes. A profile chooses either strict one-output-per-obligation or a consumer-recomputed amount partition that admits coalesced payments, with every obligation allocated exactly once.","assumptions":["The source strict-output rule covers redeemers of one S2 script hash only, not other scripts or S2 versions. No input value may count toward two obligations; ledger single-spend alone is not a value partition.","Circuit-level NotBound/TouchedOnly limits remain. A general multi-UTxO authenticated frame, role sets, cross-script allocation and partial-completion rules are PROPOSED.","No genuine intent signature is observed in the supplied proof records.","A supplied approved=true is consented to by the effect-derived required signers, and by a listed advancer if any; that is the debited account's consent, not a separate governance decision. A distinct approver must be a required signer, an advancer or an authenticated input.","SEC06-P05 PROPOSED; still needs double-satisfaction negatives across scripts and S2 versions, a coalesced positive under partition, fee-over-cap and dropped-duty cases, the approver-coverage lint, a genuine validator-checked signature in real context, and independent custody/duty/backing accounting with a backing-policy negative."],"evidenceStage":"V2 SOURCE READING; allocation policy PROPOSED per profile","sourceRefs":["ERR-08","ERR-09","ERR-10","ERR-23","ERR-24","CL-22","CL-25","CL-41","CL-48","CL-51","SEC06-P05 PROPOSED"]},{"id":"duties-backing-fees","title":"Duties, backing and fees","body":"S12 and S13 source behavior requires non-negative custody, not full backing of liabilities. S12 funds escrow at 110: partial(35) debits 37 and leaves 73 against duties of 75 (invoice 65, reserve 10), which is 2 short. Funding 112 would back one partial; each further partial charges another 2 that no duty reflects. That backing is a PROPOSED application policy. S12 has no extra transfer and no reserve-duty-return action; after finish, escrow holds 8 against reserve duty 10, and the nonzero reserve duty blocks V2 Close. S13 allocate(true,23,7,2) needs at least 23 A and 4 B for non-negative custody; 25 A and 10 B is PROPOSED full backing, and S13 has no action discharging allocation_reserve.","assumptions":["Source-only arithmetic, not execution. The V2 Close zero-duty condition is an uncompiled-source reading.","Even under the proposed 112 policy the reserve duty stays 10 and Close stays blocked; discharge or migration needs a program change.","S13 units is checked only arithmetically (23 = 3×7 + 2) and is never debited.","A program-asset fee is not debt repayment and not a network fee. Network fees, collateral, claim-output min-ADA and the V2 thread reserve (lovelace returned on Close) are separate funding items; a reserve duty is a program liability.","Delivery evidence and dispute rules remain named assumptions. Still needs independent open/partial/finish/reserve-return accounting and a backing-policy negative."],"evidenceStage":"Source arithmetic reading; full backing PROPOSED","sourceRefs":["ERR-21","ERR-22","ERR-25","ERR-28","CL-19","CL-20","CL-25","CL-26","CL-52","S12 actions","S13 action allocate","SEC06-P05 PROPOSED"]},{"id":"page-scope","title":"What the interactive page does","body":"The invoice worksheet is local browser arithmetic over the S12 fixture values; it does not execute Moriarty, produce or check a proof, or create a transaction or ledger state. The explorer’s remove and restore control shows the consumer rule; a complete Web on this page is not proof verification, acceptance or settlement. Role cards state what each transition must prove; they do not claim fifteen independently implemented proof producers, and the supplied proof evidence concerns one invoice-partial relation. DEX, issuance and loan amounts are worked examples with no supplied source program; issuance additionally needs an application mint policy, which is distinct from the C2 claim policy. Identifiers shown in the explorer are placeholders. Cost: more Strands can duplicate context hashing, range constraints, exports, issuance and verification, and whole execution may be preferable; no measurement exists.","assumptions":[],"evidenceStage":"Page scope","sourceRefs":["invoice-fixtures.json /description","scenarios.json","CONCEPT.md Optimization without assumed savings","ERR-15","CL-29","CL-30"]},{"id":"scope-and-permission","title":"Permissionless use and retained scope","body":"Moriarty is a permissionless language of provable intention: any developer may author, compile, prove, publish and deploy programs in the supported language without approval from Moriarty maintainers, an agent council, Foreman, a hosted solver or a program registry. Objective validity rules, resource costs and asset-owner authorization still apply, and application-specific access control remains expressible without becoming a deployment restriction. Optional hosted proving, relaying or indexing services are never required. Moriarty's broader target remains Midnight ZKIRv3 and native proof infrastructure; Cardano is an additional experiment and does not replace that contract or qualify Midnight. Preview-first and later Preprod end-to-end acceptance remain required for claimed targets. No claim is made that a Cardano Web ports to another target unchanged.","retainedScope":{"body":"One example does not establish compatibility. The wider language and evidence requirements remain open.","retainedFamilies":["S01","S02","S03","S04","escrow-time","interval-boundary","joint-role","oracle-observation","bilateral atomic escrow swap","constant-product exact rounding/slippage with serialized pool continuation","order execution versus timeout cancellation","two-order batch exact input coverage and injective output allocation / same-output double satisfaction","F01-origination","F02-interest-period","F03-partial-repayment-residual-liability","F04-fee-gross-net-policy","AP01","AP02","AP03","AP04"],"familiesStatus":"Requirements, not supported ports.","actus":"277 fixtures, 18 executable types, 32 taxonomy dispositions and DS01–07.","defi":"72 rows, DA01–24, held-outs, intent cases and regressions.","furtherTracks":["General financial obligations","Universal property certificates","Authenticated history","Recursion","Private handoff","Bounded multiparent PCD"],"target":"Midnight ZKIRv3 and native proof infrastructure remain the broader target; Cardano is an additional experiment.","qualification":"Preview-first and later Preprod end-to-end acceptance remain required for claimed targets.","catalogue":"235 pre-dedup review identifiers, not proven properties. Original 42/52 lists remain unrecovered. Required setup count is unknown."},"evidenceStage":"Product contract (S03) and retained scope","sourceRefs":["S03 introduction, Product boundary, Execution target and certified basis, Verification versus project process","SECURITY-ERRATA approvedDraftClaims (permissionless tooling)","learning.json /broaderScope","S07 /allRequiredScopeRetained","CONCEPT.md Evidence appendix and retained breadth"]}],"sourceRefs":[{"label":"User v2 presentation steering","document":"moriarty-webs-react-draft-20261016/USER-V2-PRESENTATION-STEERING.json","pointer":"/verbatim, /interpretation"},{"label":"Role06 security cards, glossary and invoice rules","document":"moriarty-webs-react-draft-20261016/contributions/06-security/SECURITY-COPY.json","pointer":"/cards, /glossaryClarifications, /invoiceRules"},{"label":"Security errata ERR-01 to ERR-28","document":"moriarty-webs-ten-study-20261016/security-reviewed/SECURITY-ERRATA.json","pointer":"/rows, /approvedDraftClaims"},{"label":"Invoice worksheet fixtures","document":"moriarty-webs-react-draft-20261016/contributions/04-scenarios/invoice-fixtures.json","pointer":"/opening, /partial, /fundingModes, /fixtures, /limitations"},{"label":"Five use-case scenarios","document":"moriarty-webs-react-draft-20261016/contributions/04-scenarios/scenarios.json","pointer":"/0 to /4 strands"},{"label":"Invoice source actions","document":"moriarty-webs-ten-study-20261016/sources/S12-installment-invoice.mori","pointer":"actions open, partial, finish"},{"label":"Conditional reserve source","document":"moriarty-webs-ten-study-20261016/sources/S13-conditional-reserve.mori","pointer":"actions allocate, rebate"},{"label":"Permissionless product contract","document":"reference-inputs/concept/sources/S03-MORIARTY-PRODUCT-CONTRACT.md","pointer":"Product boundary; Execution target and certified basis; Verification versus project process"},{"label":"Integrated concept","document":"reference-inputs/concept/integrated-r2/CONCEPT.md","pointer":"How an application would use a Web; Five application cases; Optimization without assumed savings; Evidence appendix and retained breadth"},{"label":"Retained broader scope","document":"moriarty-webs-react-draft-20261016/contributions/08-learning/learning.json","pointer":"/broaderScope"},{"label":"S07 invoice-partial roundtrip","document":"moriarty-webs-ten-study-20261016/sources/S07-K6-ACTUAL-ADOPTION.json","pointer":"/scope, /limitations, /allRequiredScopeRetained"},{"label":"S08 compiled C2 wrapper cases","document":"moriarty-webs-ten-study-20261016/sources/S08-K10-ACTUAL-ADOPTION.json","pointer":"/predicate, /cases, /scopeLimitations"},{"label":"Conditional T1 adoption","document":"A01 public record","pointer":"/scope, /firstBlockedBoundary"}]}},"groth16":{"kicker":"The proof system","title":"Groth16 proofs and trusted setup","intro":"Groth16 lets a prover show that a computation satisfies a fixed set of constraints without publishing its private inputs. A Strand uses it to carry a compact proof that the financial calculation follows those rules. Here is what the proof contains, what the validator checks and why setup is needed.","concepts":[{"label":"Public information","title":"The statement","body":"The statement says what is being proved. In a Web, the public inputs carry a digest of the statement bytes. The validator rebuilds those bytes from the transaction, so a correct calculation cannot be attached to a different payment. A proof alone does not authenticate an external price or document."},{"label":"Private information","title":"The witness","body":"The witness contains the other values needed to satisfy the circuit, including intermediate calculations. The proof hides those values. Anything already published in a Cardano output or the public statement remains public."},{"label":"The check","title":"Proof and verification key","body":"The proving key lets a prover create a proof from the statement and witness. The validator uses the verification key and public inputs to check a proof of three group elements with a pairing equation. Both keys are public; the secret that must be destroyed belongs to setup."}],"setupTitle":"Why there is a trusted setup","setupIntro":"Groth16 needs parameters for the exact compiled circuit. Setup derives the proving and verification keys using secret randomness. If someone retains that randomness, they can forge proofs. A common ceremony divides this work into two phases so part of it can be shared.","setupStages":[{"label":"Phase 1","title":"Parameters that circuits can share","body":"Often called powers of tau, this phase prepares parameters for a particular curve and capacity. Circuits can reuse them when their curve and format are compatible and they fit within that capacity."},{"label":"Phase 2","title":"Keys for one compiled circuit","body":"Each distinct circuit needs its own second phase. Its constraints, public-input layout and bounds determine which keys are valid. New values can reuse those keys; a changed circuit needs a new phase 2."}],"counter":{"initial":5,"min":1,"max":20,"kicker":"Try a circuit count","title":"How many setup stages are needed?","body":"Assume these circuits share a compatible curve and format, and one phase-1 parameter set is large enough for all of them. Change the count to see what can be reused.","label":"Distinct compiled circuits","decreaseLabel":"Fewer circuits","increaseLabel":"More circuits","formula":"1 shared phase 1 + {K} phase 2","result":"For {K} distinct circuits, phase 1 can be shared and phase 2 runs {K} times. These are stages, not {K} + 1 complete ceremonies.","conditions":"The count holds only if every circuit uses the same curve and format and fits the shared phase-1 capacity. Smaller circuits need no extra phase 1. Each stage yields sound keys only if its transcript verifies and at least one participant in it was honest."},"reuseTitle":"When can the same keys be used again?","reuseExamples":[{"title":"The amounts or users change","body":"The keys can be reused if the compiled circuit stays the same. New transactions and different public-input values do not, by themselves, require setup again."},{"title":"The compiled rules change","body":"A circuit with different constraints, bounds or public-input layout needs a new phase 2. A new Groth16 wrapper also has its own circuit. Aggregation does not remove setup for the base circuits."},{"title":"One bounded circuit covers several kinds of claim","body":"A parameterized circuit can cover many claims within fixed bounds. That is how a catalogue can contain more claim types than circuits. It does not make the circuit unbounded."}],"trustTitle":"What makes a ceremony trustworthy?","trust":"Proving and verification keys are public. The secret is the setup randomness, often called toxic waste. Anyone who holds it can produce verifying proofs for statements that have no witness, so it must not survive the setup. A multi-party phase is trustworthy when its transcript verifies and at least one participant in that phase was honest, under the protocol's assumptions. Every phase needs that property.","cardanoTitle":"How many setups for all of Cardano?","cardanoCount":"There is no fixed Cardano-wide total. Count one phase 2 for each distinct compiled circuit, including wrappers. Count phase 1 separately for each compatible parameter set those circuits use; an existing set can be reused if its capacity is sufficient. Claim types and architectural layers do not determine the number of circuits. A total requires a frozen inventory of compiled circuits.","technicalLinkLabel":"Read the status of Moriarty’s current test setup","sourceRefs":[{"label":"Groth, On the Size of Pairing-based Non-interactive Arguments (EUROCRYPT 2016)","url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2016/260","pointer":"§2.2 Setup(R), Sim; §3.2 construction and efficiency"},{"label":"snarkjs README","url":"https://github.com/iden3/snarkjs","pointer":"Steps 1-8 (phase 1), 15-22 (Groth16 phase 2)"},{"label":"Filecoin phase 2 attestations","url":"https://github.com/filecoin-project/phase2-attestations#description-of-trusted-setup-phases","pointer":"Description of Trusted-Setup Phases"},{"label":"Moriarty implementation status","url":"#implementation-status","pointer":"technical.trustNotes setup-keys"}]},"sevenLayers":{"kicker":"Architecture","title":"The parts of a zero-knowledge proof system","intro":"A proof is useful only if it checks the right program against the right inputs. These seven concerns explain the connection, from the financial rules a developer writes to the checks a Cardano validator performs. They influence one another; the numbers are not seven execution steps.","selectorLabel":"Choose a part of the proof system","panelLabel":"Explanation of the selected concern","artifactLabel":"Result","dependenciesLabel":"Related concerns","layers":[{"number":1,"name":"Setup","headline":"Which keys does the circuit use?","body":"Groth16 derives proving and verifying keys from secret randomness for one compiled circuit. Each distinct compiled relation needs its own phase-2 setup; the count follows circuits, not predicates or catalogue entries. Whoever keeps that randomness can forge proofs, so soundness assumes it is destroyed.","artifact":"Proving key and verifying key","dependencies":[4,5,6],"mapping":"Catalogue entries and owner intents name the verifying key.","subtitle":"Keys for the compiled circuit"},{"number":2,"name":"Language","headline":"What rules does the contract enforce?","body":"Moriarty programs state what an action may do: payment arithmetic, fees kept separate from repayment, caps an owner signs, and duties that continue. The compiler turns that source into constraints; a rule left unconstrained is a rule no proof enforces.","artifact":"Moriarty source program","dependencies":[4],"mapping":"Moriarty rules for payments, fees, caps and duties.","subtitle":"The financial program"},{"number":3,"name":"Witness","headline":"Which values satisfy those rules?","body":"The prover runs the program over its inputs and records every intermediate value. Zero-knowledge keeps this assignment hidden, but only the assignment: amounts that appear in Cardano outputs or in the statement stay public. Witness generation must not leak values through its own behavior.","artifact":"Full witness assignment","dependencies":[2,4],"mapping":"One whole-execution witness can serve several Strand roles.","subtitle":"Values the prover supplies"},{"number":4,"name":"Arithmetization","headline":"How are the rules expressed as constraints?","body":"The compiled program becomes rank-1 constraints over a prime field, which Groth16 encodes as a quadratic arithmetic program. A valid witness satisfies every constraint. The public inputs carry a statement digest; an in-circuit BLAKE2b gadget ties them to the statement bytes.","artifact":"R1CS matrices","dependencies":[2,5,6],"mapping":"Caps, fees and duty updates become constraints.","subtitle":"Equations the proof can check","plainLanguage":"The compiler translates the program into equations. A prover must supply values that satisfy all of them. Arithmetization is the name for this way of expressing the computation."},{"number":5,"name":"Proof System","headline":"How does Groth16 prove the calculation?","body":"Groth16 turns the witness into a constant-size proof checked by one pairing equation. Under an honest setup, knowledge soundness means a verifying proof shows the prover knew a satisfying witness; zero-knowledge means the proof reveals nothing beyond its statement. It never shows the statement's values describe the real ledger.","artifact":"Groth16 proof","dependencies":[1,4,6],"mapping":"Each Strand carries one proof.","subtitle":"The proof construction"},{"number":6,"name":"Cryptographic Bedrock","headline":"Which cryptographic assumptions does it rely on?","body":"A pairing-friendly elliptic curve supplies Groth16's groups and scalar field; Groth16's soundness argument holds in the generic bilinear group model. BLAKE2b collision resistance binds statement bytes to digests. Signatures authenticate owner intents and claimant releases. These primitives support the proof core and fix what it can express.","artifact":"Curve, hash and signature parameters","dependencies":[],"mapping":"Cardano scripts evaluate the pairing check and hashes.","subtitle":"Curves, hashes and signatures"},{"number":7,"name":"Verification","headline":"Does this proof belong to this transaction?","body":"A claim policy checks each Groth16 proof and mints a proof-claim output. One settlement transaction consumes the proof-claim output of every Strand the Web requires; the validator rebuilds each statement from the transaction and spent state, requires claimant signatures and enforces signed caps. Outside facts need a named issuer, authentication path and freshness limit.","artifact":"Issue and settlement transactions","dependencies":[1,5,6],"mapping":"All-or-nothing Web consumer on Cardano.","subtitle":"The connection to Cardano"}],"relationshipsTitle":"How these concerns affect one another","relationships":["Layers 4, 5 and 6 form the proof core: the scalar field, the R1CS encoding and Groth16's pairing check are chosen as one design, not three.","Setup follows the compiled relation. Changing a rule changes the circuit, and a changed circuit needs a new setup and a new verifying key.","The proof and the ledger divide the work. Layers 3 to 5 show the statement's arithmetic is correct; Layer 7 checks, from the transaction itself, that the statement describes this step.","The verifier reaches down. Cardano scripts must run the pairing check, which constrains the curve and proof system beneath them.","The numbering follows how a reader meets the system, not the order of engineering decisions; the dependency lists here sketch the main pressures, not a complete graph."],"mappingTitle":"Where each concern appears in a Web","mappingHeaders":["Concern","Moriarty example"],"credit":"This explanation uses the seven-layer framework from Proving Nothing, a book about zero-knowledge systems. The application to Moriarty is developed here.","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/CharlesHoskinson/sevenlayer/blob/2789e5a63972276e20bc6144733ef9824ced9e49/proving-nothing.md","sourceLabel":"Read the framework and its source passages","sourceRefs":[{"label":"The Seven Layers at a Glance","url":"https://github.com/CharlesHoskinson/sevenlayer/blob/2789e5a63972276e20bc6144733ef9824ced9e49/proving-nothing.md?plain=1#L272-L298","pointer":"Lines 272–298: the seven layers as interdependent organs"},{"label":"Universal versus Circuit-Specific Setups","url":"https://github.com/CharlesHoskinson/sevenlayer/blob/2789e5a63972276e20bc6144733ef9824ced9e49/proving-nothing.md?plain=1#L609-L621","pointer":"Lines 609–621: a Groth16 setup is specific to one circuit"},{"label":"The Proof Core: Layers 4, 5, and 6","url":"https://github.com/CharlesHoskinson/sevenlayer/blob/2789e5a63972276e20bc6144733ef9824ced9e49/proving-nothing.md?plain=1#L2340-L2353","pointer":"Lines 2340–2353: separate concerns, joint implementation"},{"label":"The Causal Web: Why It Is a DAG, Not a Stack","url":"https://github.com/CharlesHoskinson/sevenlayer/blob/2789e5a63972276e20bc6144733ef9824ced9e49/proving-nothing.md?plain=1#L4460-L4479","pointer":"Lines 4460–4479: upward and downward pressures between layers"},{"label":"Groth, On the Size of Pairing-based Non-interactive Arguments (EUROCRYPT 2016)","url":"https://eprint.iacr.org/2016/260","pointer":"Sections 2.2 and 3.2: Setup(R), knowledge soundness, zero-knowledge, verification equation"}]},"bookFindings":[{"id":"F1","severity":"medium","summary":"On Cardano, zero-knowledge hides only the witness. Amounts in settlement outputs and in statements the validator rebuilds are public.","evidence":"V2-COPY proofRoleExplanation and lifecycle 'settle': the validator rebuilds each statement from the transaction and reads back the actual outputs. G05 §2.2: zero-knowledge covers the witness, not the statement.","requiredFix":"Copy must not say that transaction amounts are private. This is applied in Layer 3."},{"id":"F2","severity":"low","summary":"The book and the Groth paper describe Groth16 verification differently.","evidence":"Book lines 609–621 say 'two pairing checks… three distinct pairing computations'. G05 §3.2 says verification checks a single pairing product equation using 3 pairings.","requiredFix":"Copy follows the paper ('one pairing equation') and gives no pairing counts or proof sizes. The book passage could be fixed separately."},{"id":"F3","severity":"low","summary":"Groth16 knowledge soundness is proven in the generic bilinear group model, not from discrete-log hardness alone. Claimant bytes are not an authenticated prover identity.","evidence":"G05 Theorem 3. V2-COPY binding-chain assumptions.","requiredFix":"Layer 6 names the generic bilinear group model. It says signatures authenticate claimant releases and does not call them prover identities."}],"catalogueText":"{\n  \"schema\": \"moriarty-cardano-property-catalogue/1.2\",\n  \"catalogueId\": \"moriarty-cardano-property-catalogue\",\n  \"catalogueVersion\": \"1.2-draft-20261015\",\n  \"author\": {\n    \"model\": \"claude-opus-5-5\",\n    \"role\": \"v1.2 repair source author for review fdecbe41 B1-B3 (fresh context; same model id as the v1/v1.1 authors, the reviewers and the falsifier author, so context independence only). Nothing in this document grants authority; it is a candidate pending a fresh independent full-candidate and result review.\"\n  },\n  \"scope\": \"Version-1.2 draft of the Moriarty Cardano property catalogue. Carries all 143 v1.1 rows (including the 137 v1 rows and the 80 v0 rows) byte-identically under MCJ/1 with the same 89 current heads; no row is added, removed, renamed or re-versioned, so every row and predicate digest is unchanged. The edition records the review fdecbe41 checker repairs (consumer-declared audit grants, full-match typing, typed nested members) in its schema descriptions. Research vocabulary and typed statement source for a UNIT checker only. Row count is not an exhaustiveness or coverage claim. No row is a bridge row. Mutable status prose lives in the non-digested NOTES file.\",\n  \"base\": {\n    \"catalogueVersion\": \"0-draft-20261015\",\n    \"locator\": \"cardano-proof-catalogue-20261015/CARDANO-PROPERTY-CATALOGUE.json\",\n    \"fileBytes\": 131539,\n    \"fileSha256\": \"a283133b87745999be91e797d205f05964793642f2201766111d57af153fe2b9\",\n    \"rowsDigestSha256\": \"a72bccc1c6539394685a10b8095bbd88a7c206746e985a9c21920a9dd0b89557\",\n    \"rowsDigestRule\": \"sha256 of JSON rows array with sorted keys, separators (',',':'), UTF-8\",\n    \"rowCount\": 80,\n    \"immutability\": \"v0 bytes and every v0 row are immutable; the checker compares each v0 row under MCJ/1 and recomputes the v0 rows digest under both the v0 rule and MCJ/1\"\n  },\n  \"v1Base\": {\n    \"catalogueVersion\": \"1-draft-20261015\",\n    \"locator\": \"cardano-catalogue-v1-implementation-20261015/CARDANO-PROPERTY-CATALOGUE-v1.json\",\n    \"fileBytes\": 290716,\n    \"fileSha256\": \"4cc79c59cd9adc2ba78c64102f0850d43117522b2ea56e6c0e26a612bd9198c4\",\n    \"catalogueManifestDigest\": \"4dbe36a58a04f09e13461a28a96b43b1dc2d5e1c8043835849df0ef4c9093498\",\n    \"rowCount\": 137,\n    \"immutability\": \"v1 bytes and every v1 row are immutable; the checker compares each v1 row under MCJ/1 with the pinned v1 document\"\n  },\n  \"v11Base\": {\n    \"catalogueVersion\": \"1.1-draft-20261015\",\n    \"locator\": \"cardano-catalogue-v11-hardening-20261015/candidate/data/CARDANO-PROPERTY-CATALOGUE-v1.1.json\",\n    \"fileBytes\": 325852,\n    \"fileSha256\": \"5189c5e08518601e6eb608df2886f784a0b16390fb0e126da86c4ff81523e9d5\",\n    \"catalogueManifestDigest\": \"c856d17bb166d95bcb4315e4a58c9451de09d9ab09fff7532f46c5f0be28bee2\",\n    \"rowCount\": 143,\n    \"immutability\": \"v1.1 bytes and every v1.1 row are immutable; the checker compares each v1.1 row under MCJ/1 with the pinned v1.1 document (CAT_V11_ROW_MISSING / CAT_V11_ROW_ALTERED)\"\n  },\n  \"lineage\": {\n    \"predecessorVersion\": \"1.1-draft-20261015\",\n    \"successorVersion\": \"1.2-draft-20261015\",\n    \"rowCarriage\": \"all 143 v1.1 rows carried byte-identically in v1.1 order; 89 current heads; no added, removed, renamed or re-versioned row; every row_digest and predicate_digest equals the v1.1 manifest's; introducedIn keeps the edition that first carried each row\",\n    \"renames\": [\n      {\n        \"item\": \"catalogue document schema\",\n        \"from\": \"moriarty-cardano-property-catalogue/1.1\",\n        \"to\": \"moriarty-cardano-property-catalogue/1.2\",\n        \"note\": \"members v11Base and lineage added; every other member keeps its v1.1 shape\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"item\": \"catalogueVersion\",\n        \"from\": \"1.1-draft-20261015\",\n        \"to\": \"1.2-draft-20261015\",\n        \"note\": \"bundles naming this edition carry catalogue_version 1.2-draft-20261015; a 1.1 document stays readable as a legacy edition, validated only under the repaired rules\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"item\": \"consumer policy schema\",\n        \"from\": \"moriarty-cardano-consumer-policy/1 (accepted_auditors = scope-less {auditor_id, report_sha256} pairs)\",\n        \"to\": \"moriarty-cardano-consumer-policy/2 (accepted_auditors = consumer-declared grants {auditor_id, report_sha256, verdict, scopes, covers_vk_digests, predicate_refs})\",\n        \"note\": \"policy /1 remains valid only with accepted_auditors == []; a non-empty /1 list is refused (CTX_POLICY_SCHEMA), never reinterpreted\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"unchanged\": [\n      {\n        \"item\": \"canonicalization\",\n        \"identity\": \"MCJ/1; mcj.py byte-identical to v1.1; a scoped profile with no full RFC 8785 claim\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"item\": \"SemanticEntry projection and predicate_digest rule\",\n        \"identity\": \"moriarty-cardano-semantic-entry/1\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"item\": \"AdmissionRecord format\",\n        \"identity\": \"moriarty-cardano-admission-record/1.1; audit_refs keep their shape and earn credit only via consumer grants\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"item\": \"proof bundle format\",\n        \"identity\": \"moriarty-cardano-proof-bundle/1.1\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"item\": \"manifest format\",\n        \"identity\": \"moriarty-cardano-catalogue-manifest/1.1\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"item\": \"profiles, vocabularies, semantic table, setup classes, identity layers, pattern inventory and families\",\n        \"identity\": \"equal to v1.1 under MCJ/1\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"item\": \"settlement boundary\",\n        \"identity\": \"UNSUPPORTED: no adopted S_settle0/C0 ABI\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"repairs\": [\n      {\n        \"id\": \"B1\",\n        \"source\": \"independent review REPORT.md sha256 fdecbe4137ca3bf8b1b642825bc3532efbaa6470e863739476734e7d3f8ffc91\",\n        \"summary\": \"audit credit = record scopes intersected with consumer grant scopes, only where the grant covers the record's vk_digest and predicate_ref; consumer dispute grants refuse whether or not the record cites them; uncited grants and ungranted refs count for nothing; the deny-list is kept\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"id\": \"B2\",\n        \"source\": \"independent review REPORT.md sha256 fdecbe4137ca3bf8b1b642825bc3532efbaa6470e863739476734e7d3f8ffc91\",\n        \"summary\": \"every typed string validated by re.fullmatch over ASCII-only classes; decimal strings length-bounded; predicate refs typed as versioned ids; pinned content compared by MCJ/1 bytes (true is not 1)\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"id\": \"B3\",\n        \"source\": \"independent review REPORT.md sha256 fdecbe4137ca3bf8b1b642825bc3532efbaa6470e863739476734e7d3f8ffc91\",\n        \"summary\": \"lint and pattern-family checks iterate only type-validated containers; every document member typed; no library exception for MCJ-valid input; CLI stdout is ASCII JSON\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"notClaimed\": [\n      \"no row of the bridge's missing 42 Cardano / 52 Midnight catalogues is recovered, renamed or reconstructed; MCP ids remain a separate namespace\",\n      \"no proof, VK, setup, signature, audit, ledger state or deployment is verified; grants and audit references are unsigned declarations\",\n      \"no full RFC 8785 conformance, no cryptographic authentication and no ledger truth\",\n      \"rows are an inventory, not approved predicates; all 20 pattern families remain open\",\n      \"universal certificates, execution claims and authenticated-ledger claims stay distinct claim kinds; no unverified relation becomes admitted\"\n    ]\n  },\n  \"bridgeCatalogueRelation\": {\n    \"recoveredBridgeRows\": 0,\n    \"statement\": \"None of these rows is a row of the bridge's missing 42 Cardano / 52 Midnight catalogues. Ids use the separate MCP namespace. See BRIDGE-LIST-CROSSCHECK.md.\",\n    \"missingFiles\": [\n      \"verified-claim-catalog-42.md\",\n      \"midnight-proof-claim-catalog-52.md\",\n      \"cardano-prior-epoch-zk-proof-categories.md\"\n    ],\n    \"v1Statement\": \"MCP ids are a separate namespace; none recovers, reconstructs, renames or splits any row of the missing bridge 42 Cardano / 52 Midnight catalogues. Status 'recovered' is refused by the checker.\"\n  },\n  \"canonicalization\": {\n    \"profile\": \"MCJ/1\",\n    \"domain\": [\n      \"object with string keys\",\n      \"array\",\n      \"string of Unicode scalar values\",\n      \"integer with |n| <= 2^53-1\",\n      \"true\",\n      \"false\",\n      \"null\"\n    ],\n    \"refused\": [\n      \"any float or fraction/exponent number form, including 1.0 and -0.0\",\n      \"NaN, Infinity\",\n      \"integers outside the range\",\n      \"duplicate object keys\",\n      \"lone surrogates\",\n      \"invalid UTF-8\",\n      \"UTF-8 BOM\",\n      \"non-string keys and non-JSON host types\",\n      \"nesting deeper than 64 containers (MCJ_DEPTH; v1.1, review A7)\"\n    ],\n    \"serialization\": [\n      \"no insignificant whitespace\",\n      \"object members sorted by UTF-16 code units of the key\",\n      \"escape \\\" and \\\\, U+0008 \\\\b, U+0009 \\\\t, U+000A \\\\n, U+000C \\\\f, U+000D \\\\r, other U+0000..U+001F as lowercase \\\\u00xx; all other characters literal\",\n      \"integers in shortest decimal form\",\n      \"UTF-8 output\"\n    ],\n    \"jcsRelation\": \"Intended to coincide with RFC 8785 inside this domain [EXT: RFC text not captured or inspected; no reviewed JCS implementation installed]. Not a full-JCS claim. Python json sort_keys differs from MCJ/1 on key order whenever keys mix non-BMP characters with BMP characters above U+D7FF (vector V3).\",\n    \"largeIntegers\": \"Values that may exceed 2^53-1 (lovelace, UInt128 bounds) are canonical decimal strings.\",\n    \"implementation\": \"src/mcpcat/mcj.py\",\n    \"secondCanonicalizer\": \"REQUIRED before any ABI depends on MCJ/1 (audit V1 condition (a)): an independent implementation must reproduce all 27 vectors and the v1 manifest digest 4dbe36a5...3498. The v1.1 task's falsifiers/harness/mcj_alt.py reproduces them but is author-correlated and does not satisfy this requirement.\",\n    \"scopeLimit\": \"MCJ/1 is host-side only; it is never the Plutus Data or in-circuit statement encoding (audit V1 condition (c)).\"\n  },\n  \"digestRules\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"row_digest\",\n      \"rule\": \"SHA-256(MCJ/1(row)); listed only in the separate manifest\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"predicate_digest\",\n      \"rule\": \"BLAKE2b-256(MCJ/1(SemanticEntry)); SemanticEntry is the deterministic projection in schemas.semanticEntry; listed only in the manifest; statements bind this per-row digest, never a whole-catalogue digest\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"admission_digest\",\n      \"rule\": \"BLAKE2b-256(MCJ/1(AdmissionRecord)); records live outside the catalogue\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"catalogue manifest digest\",\n      \"rule\": \"SHA-256(MCJ/1(this entire document)); stored only in the separate manifest (CATALOGUE-v1.2-MANIFEST.json for this edition). This document contains no digest of itself, of its rows or of its manifest (checker code CAT_SELF_DIGEST); the only digests it holds are of OTHER documents: base.rowsDigestSha256 (v0 rows), v1Base.catalogueManifestDigest and v11Base.catalogueManifestDigest\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"v0 rows digest\",\n      \"rule\": \"unchanged historical field; SHA-256 of v0 rows under the v0 rule (Python json sort_keys, separators , :, ensure_ascii False); equals MCJ/1 on the v0 rows because they are all ASCII and contain no numbers\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"record_log_digest\",\n      \"rule\": \"BLAKE2b-256(MCJ/1(ordered list of AdmissionRecords)); pinned by each consumer policy (review A2)\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"statusVocabulary\": {\n    \"recovered\": \"(a) row recovered from the original bridge catalogue\",\n    \"observed-now\": \"(b) provider-observed in this task with Scrapling; not a proof, not authenticated consensus\",\n    \"recorded-external-unverified\": \"(b) recorded as executed in an inspected repository page; not re-executed or verified here\",\n    \"planned-sibling-experiment\": \"(b-pending) frozen plan in the sibling experiment directory; no result existed at read time\",\n    \"source-backed-candidate\": \"(c) property family supported by inspected sources; construction not implemented\",\n    \"conditional-construction\": \"(c) construction plausible only under named unmet conditions\",\n    \"research-only\": \"(c) research direction; costs or semantics unestablished\",\n    \"unsupported-now\": \"(d) cannot be claimed with currently available primitives/anchors\",\n    \"refused\": \"(d) anti-pattern; must be refused by any consumer\",\n    \"experiment-recorded-unaudited\": \"(b) experiment result recorded by its author; independent audit pending; not approved; never a ledger, deployment or production claim\",\n    \"experiment-observed-audited\": \"(b) experiment result accepted by a named independent audit (row must carry auditRef); still not a ledger, deployment or production claim\",\n    \"experiment-disputed\": \"(b) experiment result rejected or qualified by a named independent audit (row must carry auditRef); claim text not upgraded\"\n  },\n  \"statusCategory\": {\n    \"recovered\": \"a\",\n    \"observed-now\": \"b\",\n    \"recorded-external-unverified\": \"b\",\n    \"planned-sibling-experiment\": \"b-pending\",\n    \"source-backed-candidate\": \"c\",\n    \"conditional-construction\": \"c\",\n    \"research-only\": \"c\",\n    \"unsupported-now\": \"d\",\n    \"refused\": \"d\",\n    \"experiment-recorded-unaudited\": \"b\",\n    \"experiment-observed-audited\": \"b\",\n    \"experiment-disputed\": \"b\"\n  },\n  \"categoryVocabulary\": {\n    \"a\": \"recovered from the original bridge catalogue (no such row exists; recovery is forbidden by the bridge contract)\",\n    \"b\": \"observed or recorded, not demonstrated\",\n    \"b-pending\": \"planned experiment; no result at read time\",\n    \"c\": \"candidate or conditional construction; not implemented\",\n    \"d\": \"unsupported now or refused\"\n  },\n  \"claimKindVocabulary\": {\n    \"computation\": \"Computation about provided/committed data (true of the bytes/values, not of Cardano by itself)\",\n    \"ledger-current-context\": \"Fact the Cardano ledger enforces for the transaction being validated (relative to the fork it lands in)\",\n    \"ledger-historical-anchored\": \"Fact about past Cardano state, valid only relative to a named anchor and its trust assumptions\",\n    \"universal-property\": \"For-all property of a program/compiler artifact; a certificate, not one execution\",\n    \"verifier-property\": \"Property of the verifier/encoding layer that every proof-bearing claim depends on\",\n    \"consensus-finality\": \"Chain selection / settlement / finality statement\",\n    \"disclosure\": \"Privacy or disclosure property\",\n    \"observation\": \"Provider observation recorded by this task\",\n    \"anti-pattern\": \"Refused construction\",\n    \"external-record\": \"Author- or third-party-reported record of an execution or deployment; never a ledger claim and never settleable\",\n    \"derived-history\": \"History claim derived from universal certificates plus a ledger observation; fork-relative and void on rollback past the observed point\",\n    \"external-attestation\": \"Claim that rests on a named external attester (issuer identity, backing, redemption); not a ledger fact\"\n  },\n  \"claimKindClasses\": {\n    \"observationKinds\": [\n      \"external-record\",\n      \"observation\"\n    ],\n    \"observationStatuses\": [\n      \"observed-now\",\n      \"recorded-external-unverified\"\n    ],\n    \"experimentStatuses\": [\n      \"experiment-disputed\",\n      \"experiment-observed-audited\",\n      \"experiment-recorded-unaudited\"\n    ],\n    \"auditRequiredStatuses\": [\n      \"experiment-disputed\",\n      \"experiment-observed-audited\"\n    ],\n    \"admissibleKinds\": [\n      \"computation\",\n      \"universal-property\"\n    ],\n    \"rules\": [\n      \"claimKind anti-pattern <=> status refused\",\n      \"status observed-now or recorded-external-unverified => claimKind observation or external-record\",\n      \"claimKind observation or external-record => status observed/recorded or an experiment status\",\n      \"a successor of an observation/record row keeps an observation kind (no observation upgrade)\",\n      \"experiment-observed-audited and experiment-disputed require auditRef\",\n      \"violations on superseded immutable v0 rows are reported as historical warnings; anywhere else they are errors\",\n      \"row status is descriptive: it may deny admission (denyingStatuses) but never grants admission or raises an evidence cap (review A4)\",\n      \"a kind or category change across supersession in a v1.1 row requires kindChange/categoryChange {from, to, reason}; every change, including v1's, is reported (review A8)\",\n      \"SemanticEntry text of v1.1-introduced computation/universal-property rows carries no status, setup, key or provenance words (review A5)\",\n      \"v1.2 adds no row; rows introduced in 1.1 or later keep error-level semantic-text lint and kind/category-change obligations in every edition; introducedIn names the edition that first carried a row\"\n    ],\n    \"denyingStatuses\": [\n      \"planned-sibling-experiment\",\n      \"recovered\",\n      \"refused\",\n      \"unsupported-now\"\n    ]\n  },\n  \"familyTitles\": {\n    \"OBS\": \"Provider observations made by this task (category b, not proofs)\",\n    \"REC\": \"External execution records (category b, not re-verified)\",\n    \"PLN\": \"Sibling experiment predicates (planned; no result at read time)\",\n    \"CTX\": \"Current-transaction context facts the ledger enforces\",\n    \"LIN\": \"eUTxO linearity, thread and replay structure\",\n    \"EXE\": \"Computation and execution proofs over committed or provided data\",\n    \"PROP\": \"Universal property certificates (not per-transaction proofs)\",\n    \"VER\": \"Verifier, encoding and identity properties\",\n    \"LED\": \"Historical, anchored and consensus claims\",\n    \"AST\": \"Asset identity\",\n    \"META\": \"Metadata\",\n    \"GOV\": \"Governance history\",\n    \"STK\": \"Stake and reward history\",\n    \"PCD\": \"Recursive and private composition\",\n    \"XCH\": \"Cross-chain consumption\",\n    \"UNS\": \"Universal-claim limits\",\n    \"REF\": \"Refused anti-patterns\"\n  },\n  \"statementFieldVocabulary\": {\n    \"types\": [\n      \"Bytes\",\n      \"Bytes28\",\n      \"Bytes32\",\n      \"Int\",\n      \"OutRef\",\n      \"TxId\"\n    ],\n    \"sources\": [\n      \"circuit-constant\",\n      \"statement-public\",\n      \"validator-derived-context\",\n      \"validator-supplied-admission\"\n    ],\n    \"forbiddenSources\": [\n      \"prover-supplied-scalar\",\n      \"redeemer-supplied-scalar\"\n    ],\n    \"semanticAttributes\": [\n      \"digest_of\",\n      \"role\",\n      \"tx_relation\",\n      \"index_of\"\n    ],\n    \"contextItems\": [\n      \"own-outref\",\n      \"pre-datum-hash\",\n      \"pre-existing-outref-by-role\",\n      \"role-designated-effects\",\n      \"validity-range\"\n    ],\n    \"semanticTable\": [\n      {\n        \"attribute\": \"digest_of\",\n        \"value\": \"admission_digest\",\n        \"types\": [\n          \"Bytes32\"\n        ],\n        \"sources\": [\n          \"validator-supplied-admission\"\n        ],\n        \"contextItems\": [],\n        \"vkDependent\": true\n      },\n      {\n        \"attribute\": \"digest_of\",\n        \"value\": \"post-snapshot\",\n        \"types\": [\n          \"Bytes32\"\n        ],\n        \"sources\": [\n          \"statement-public\"\n        ],\n        \"contextItems\": [],\n        \"vkDependent\": false\n      },\n      {\n        \"attribute\": \"digest_of\",\n        \"value\": \"pre-snapshot\",\n        \"types\": [\n          \"Bytes32\"\n        ],\n        \"sources\": [\n          \"statement-public\"\n        ],\n        \"contextItems\": [],\n        \"vkDependent\": false\n      },\n      {\n        \"attribute\": \"digest_of\",\n        \"value\": \"pre_datum_hash\",\n        \"types\": [\n          \"Bytes32\"\n        ],\n        \"sources\": [\n          \"validator-derived-context\"\n        ],\n        \"contextItems\": [\n          \"pre-datum-hash\"\n        ],\n        \"vkDependent\": false\n      },\n      {\n        \"attribute\": \"digest_of\",\n        \"value\": \"predicate_digest\",\n        \"types\": [\n          \"Bytes32\"\n        ],\n        \"sources\": [\n          \"circuit-constant\"\n        ],\n        \"contextItems\": [],\n        \"vkDependent\": false\n      },\n      {\n        \"attribute\": \"digest_of\",\n        \"value\": \"program_hash\",\n        \"types\": [\n          \"Bytes32\"\n        ],\n        \"sources\": [\n          \"circuit-constant\"\n        ],\n        \"contextItems\": [],\n        \"vkDependent\": false\n      },\n      {\n        \"attribute\": \"digest_of\",\n        \"value\": \"raw_source_sha256\",\n        \"types\": [\n          \"Bytes32\"\n        ],\n        \"sources\": [\n          \"circuit-constant\"\n        ],\n        \"contextItems\": [],\n        \"vkDependent\": false\n      },\n      {\n        \"attribute\": \"digest_of\",\n        \"value\": \"role-designated-effects\",\n        \"types\": [\n          \"Bytes32\"\n        ],\n        \"sources\": [\n          \"validator-derived-context\"\n        ],\n        \"contextItems\": [\n          \"role-designated-effects\"\n        ],\n        \"vkDependent\": false\n      },\n      {\n        \"attribute\": \"digest_of\",\n        \"value\": \"tem